window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"MS-1":{"unit":"MS-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01","title":"Musculoskeletal Embryology","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the musculoskeletal system from the embryo to the adult layout. It covers how somites build the vertebrae, ribs, back muscles, and dermis; why each vertebral body is assembled from two sclerotome halves; how signaling centers shape the limb bud; why the limbs rotate in opposite directions; how bone forms by intramembranous and endochondral ossification; and how joints and pharyngeal arch muscles arise. Board questions rarely ask for a date alone. They give an adult finding, such as a nerve supply, a dermatome pattern, a short-limbed skeleton, or an extra digit, and ask you to work back to the embryonic step that explains it.","prerequisites":["Gastrulation and the three germ layers, including paraxial, intermediate, and lateral plate mesoderm","Notochord and neural tube formation and the role of sonic hedgehog in the neural tube","Basic structure of a spinal nerve: roots, dorsal and ventral rami","Hyaline cartilage and bone histology: chondrocytes, osteoblasts, osteoclasts, osteoid","Signaling families: FGF, Wnt, hedgehog, BMP, and HOX transcription factors"],"connections":[{"to":"Back and spinal cord anatomy","how":"Resegmentation explains why spinal nerves exit at disc level and why there are eight cervical nerves but seven cervical vertebrae."},{"to":"Upper and lower limb anatomy","how":"Limb rotation and the dorsal and ventral muscle masses predict which nerve supplies each compartment and why the lower limb dermatomes spiral."},{"to":"Bone physiology and metabolic bone disease","how":"The growth plate built during endochondral ossification is the site affected in achondroplasia and in disorders of linear growth."},{"to":"Head and neck anatomy and the cranial nerves","how":"The pharyngeal arch of origin predicts the motor nerve of each muscle of mastication, facial expression, pharynx, and larynx."},{"to":"Teratology and medical genetics","how":"Thalidomide, HOX mutations, FGFR3 mutation, and RUNX2 loss show how a single signal or gene produces a predictable skeletal defect."}],"terms":[{"term":"Somite","def":"A paired, segmental block of paraxial mesoderm beside the neural tube that forms sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome."},{"term":"Sclerotome","def":"The ventromedial part of a somite that migrates around the notochord and neural tube to form vertebrae and ribs."},{"term":"Myotome","def":"The part of a somite that forms skeletal muscle; clinically, the group of muscles supplied by one spinal nerve segment."},{"term":"Dermatome","def":"Embryologically, the part of a somite that forms dermis of the back; clinically, the skin area supplied by one spinal nerve."},{"term":"Epaxial and hypaxial","def":"The dorsal (back muscle) and ventral (body wall and limb muscle) divisions of the myotome, supplied by dorsal and ventral rami."},{"term":"Resegmentation","def":"Formation of each vertebral body from the caudal half of one sclerotome and the cranial half of the next."},{"term":"Apical ectodermal ridge (AER)","def":"Thickened ectoderm at the distal rim of the limb bud whose FGFs drive proximal to distal outgrowth."},{"term":"Zone of polarizing activity (ZPA)","def":"Posterior limb bud mesenchyme that secretes sonic hedgehog to pattern the anterior to posterior (thumb to little finger) axis."},{"term":"Preaxial border","def":"The cranial border of the early limb bud, which carries the radius and thumb or the tibia and big toe."},{"term":"Endochondral ossification","def":"Bone formation in which a hyaline cartilage model is formed first and then replaced by bone."},{"term":"Interzone","def":"A band of dense mesenchyme between two cartilage models that forms a joint instead of cartilage."},{"term":"Pharyngeal arch","def":"A paired bar of mesenchyme in the embryonic neck that contains neural crest skeleton, muscle, an artery, and its own cranial nerve."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C01","obj":"MS-1.1","title":"Somites and their derivatives","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Somites are paired blocks of paraxial mesoderm that form on each side of the neural tube and notochord. The first pair appears in the future occipital region at about day 20, and new pairs bud off in a cranial to caudal order at about three pairs per day until the end of week 5, giving about 42 to 44 pairs. The caudal-most pairs later regress. Each boundary forms on a timer: a clock of oscillating Notch and Wnt signaling in the presomitic mesoderm meets a receding FGF gradient, and a new somite separates each time the two coincide. This periodic origin is why vertebrae, ribs, back muscles, and spinal nerves are segmental."},{"type":"p","text":"Each somite then divides according to signals from its neighbors. Sonic hedgehog (Shh) from the notochord and the floor plate of the neural tube induces the ventromedial part to become sclerotome, which expresses PAX1, loses its epithelial arrangement, and migrates around the notochord and neural tube. Wnt signals from the dorsal neural tube and surface ectoderm keep the dorsolateral part as dermomyotome, which expresses PAX3 and gives rise to the myotome, the source of skeletal muscle, and the dermatome, the source of the dermis of the back."},{"type":"flow","title":"From paraxial mesoderm to adult derivatives","steps":["Paraxial mesoderm segments into somites from about day 20","Notochord and floor plate Shh induce ventromedial sclerotome","Sclerotome surrounds notochord and neural tube: vertebrae, ribs","Dorsal neural tube Wnt maintains dorsolateral dermomyotome","Dermomyotome forms myotome (muscle) and dermatome (back dermis)","Myotome splits into epaxial and hypaxial divisions"],"caption":"Signals from the notochord and neural tube decide which part of each somite becomes skeleton, muscle, or dermis."},{"type":"p","text":"Muscle keeps the nerve of the segment it came from. Each spinal nerve divides into a dorsal ramus, which enters the epaxial (dorsomedial) muscle mass, and a ventral ramus, which follows the hypaxial (ventrolateral) mass into the body wall and limbs. Epaxial muscle becomes the deep, intrinsic back muscles such as erector spinae and transversospinalis. Hypaxial muscle becomes the intercostal and abdominal wall muscles, the prevertebral and scalene muscles, and, after its precursor cells migrate into the limb buds, all limb muscle. The limb bones, tendons, and connective tissue come instead from lateral plate mesoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"This explains a classic distinction. The superficial muscles on the back, such as trapezius, latissimus dorsi, and the rhomboids, are not epaxial; they migrated onto the back and are supplied by ventral rami of the brachial plexus or, for trapezius, by the accessory nerve. Only the deep muscles of the vertebral column are supplied by dorsal rami. Because segmental innervation is kept, injury to one spinal nerve produces a predictable band of sensory loss (its dermatome) and weakness of a predictable muscle group (its myotome)."},{"type":"table","title":"Somite parts, adult derivatives, and nerve supply","cols":["Part","Adult derivatives","Nerve supply"],"rows":[["Sclerotome","Vertebral bodies and arches, ribs, annulus fibrosus, part of the occipital bone","Not applicable"],["Dermatome (embryonic)","Dermis of the skin of the neck and back","Cutaneous branches of dorsal rami"],["Epaxial myotome","Deep back muscles: erector spinae, transversospinalis, splenius, suboccipital","Dorsal rami"],["Hypaxial myotome","Limb, intercostal, abdominal wall, scalene, prevertebral, and infrahyoid muscles","Ventral rami"],["Occipital somite myotomes","Most tongue muscles","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"]],"caption":"Limb dermis, bone, and tendon are not somite derivatives; they come from lateral plate mesoderm."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C02","obj":"MS-1.2","title":"Resegmentation and the intervertebral disc","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sclerotome cells that surround the notochord form the vertebral bodies, those that surround the neural tube form the vertebral arches, and in the thoracic region the costal processes grow out to become ribs. A vertebra does not come from a single sclerotome. Each sclerotome splits into a loosely packed cranial half and a densely packed caudal half, and the caudal half of one sclerotome fuses with the cranial half of the next to form one vertebral body. This is resegmentation. Cells at the junction between the two original halves do not join either body; they fill the space between adjacent bodies and form the annulus fibrosus of the intervertebral disc."},{"type":"p","text":"Resegmentation solves a mechanical problem. The myotomes do not shift, so after resegmentation each muscle segment attaches to two adjacent vertebrae and crosses the disc between them, which is what allows segmental muscles to move the spine. For the same reason the spinal nerve, which grew toward its own myotome, now lies at the level of the disc and leaves through the intervertebral foramen, and the intersegmental arteries, which ran between somites, now cross the middle of each vertebral body."},{"type":"flow","title":"Resegmentation of the sclerotomes","steps":["Sclerotome cells surround the notochord and neural tube","Each sclerotome splits into loose cranial and dense caudal halves","Caudal half fuses with the next cranial half: one vertebral body","Cells between the original halves form the annulus fibrosus","Notochord regresses in bodies, persists between them as nucleus pulposus","Myotomes now span discs; spinal nerves lie at disc level"],"caption":"Vertebrae end up offset by half a segment from the myotomes and spinal nerves."},{"type":"p","text":"The same shift explains spinal nerve numbering. The cranial half of the first cervical sclerotome fuses with the caudal half of the last occipital sclerotome and is incorporated into the occipital bone, so the first cervical nerve emerges between the skull and the atlas. There are therefore eight cervical nerves but seven cervical vertebrae: nerves C1 to C7 exit above the vertebra of the same number, C8 exits between C7 and T1, and from T1 downward each nerve exits below its numbered vertebra."},{"type":"p","text":"The notochord is squeezed out of the developing vertebral bodies, where it regresses, but persists and expands between them as the gelatinous nucleus pulposus, which the annulus fibrosus then encircles. The large notochordal cells largely disappear from the nucleus during childhood and are replaced by smaller chondrocyte-like cells. Notochordal rests left along the axis, most often in the sacrococcygeal region and the clivus at the skull base, can give rise to chordoma, a slow-growing malignant tumor of vacuolated (physaliferous) cells that express brachyury, a notochordal transcription factor."},{"type":"table","title":"Errors of vertebral development","cols":["Defect","Embryonic error","Result"],"rows":[["Hemivertebra","One lateral half of a vertebral body fails to form","Wedge-shaped vertebra and congenital scoliosis"],["Klippel-Feil syndrome","Failed segmentation of cervical somites","Fused cervical vertebrae, short neck, low posterior hairline, limited neck motion"],["Spina bifida occulta","Vertebral arches fail to fuse dorsally, usually at L5 or S1","Skin-covered defect, sometimes a tuft of hair; cord usually normal"],["Cervical rib","Costal process of C7 enlarges","May compress the lower brachial plexus or subclavian artery"],["Chordoma","Notochordal rest persists","Malignant tumor of the clivus or sacrum"]],"caption":"Each defect traces to one step: somite segmentation, sclerotome growth, arch fusion, costal process growth, or notochord regression."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A fetal ultrasound shows a hemivertebra at T8 in which only the left half of the body formed. Which way will the spine curve?","explain":"The left side of that segment has a vertebral half and the right side has none, so the left side is taller. The spine bends away from the taller side, producing a scoliosis convex to the left at that level, and the curve tends to worsen with growth because the left half keeps growing.","check":"The resegmentation flow and the defect table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C03","obj":"MS-1.3","title":"Limb bud signaling and limb defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Limb buds appear near the end of week 4 as outpocketings of the ventrolateral body wall, the upper limb first and the lower limb one to two days later. Each bud is a core of mesenchyme from the somatic layer of lateral plate mesoderm covered by surface ectoderm. Three signaling centers pattern its three axes, and HOX genes give each segment its identity."},{"type":"p","text":"At the distal rim, mesenchymal FGF10 induces the ectoderm to thicken into the apical ectodermal ridge (AER). The AER secretes FGFs, including FGF8 and FGF4, that keep the mesenchyme beneath it (the progress zone) dividing and undifferentiated. In the classic model, cells that leave this zone early form proximal structures and cells that leave late form distal ones, so the limb is laid down proximal to distal: arm or thigh, then forearm or leg, then hand or foot. Loss of the AER stops outgrowth, giving amelia (absent limb) or meromelia (partial absence)."},{"type":"p","text":"The zone of polarizing activity (ZPA), mesenchyme at the posterior (postaxial) border, secretes sonic hedgehog (Shh). Digit identity depends on how much Shh a cell receives and for how long: the little finger side receives the most, and the thumb forms without it. If Shh is also made at the anterior border, by a graft or by mutation of its limb enhancer (the ZRS), a second gradient produces extra anterior digits: preaxial polydactyly, sometimes a mirror-image duplication. The AER and ZPA sustain each other, which couples growth to pattern. The third center, WNT7A in the dorsal ectoderm, induces LMX1B in dorsal mesenchyme to make dorsal structures; LMX1B mutation causes nail-patella syndrome, with small or absent nails and patellae."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The AER and ZPA maintain each other","steps":["AER FGFs maintain Shh expression in the ZPA","ZPA Shh induces the BMP antagonist gremlin in mesenchyme","Gremlin blocks BMP, which would shut down the AER","AER keeps secreting FGFs, maintaining Shh"],"caption":"The loop ends as the bud grows and gremlin falls, so outgrowth and patterning stop together."},{"type":"p","text":"HOX genes are transcription factors expressed in nested domains along the limb. Genes at the 5 prime end of the HOXA and HOXD clusters (paralog groups 9 to 13) switch on progressively later and more distally, and HOX13 genes mark the hand and foot. HOXD13 mutation causes synpolydactyly, with fused digits and an extra digit in the web, and HOXA13 mutation causes hand-foot-genital syndrome. Digits separate by BMP-driven apoptosis between them; failure gives syndactyly."},{"type":"p","text":"Thalidomide shows that timing decides the defect. Exposure while the limb buds form and grow out, about days 24 to 36 after fertilization, causes limb reduction defects, most characteristically phocomelia, in which the hands or feet attach close to the trunk because the long bones are absent or rudimentary. The leading explanation is that thalidomide binds cereblon, part of an E3 ubiquitin ligase, redirecting it to degrade transcription factors such as SALL4 that limb outgrowth needs. Later exposure spares the limbs."},{"type":"table","title":"Limb signaling centers and their defects","cols":["Center","Signal","Axis or role","Defect when disrupted"],"rows":[["Apical ectodermal ridge","FGF8, FGF4","Proximal to distal outgrowth","Amelia or meromelia"],["Zone of polarizing activity","Sonic hedgehog","Anterior to posterior (digit identity)","Ectopic anterior Shh: preaxial polydactyly"],["Dorsal ectoderm","WNT7A, then LMX1B","Dorsal to ventral","Nail-patella syndrome (LMX1B)"],["Limb mesenchyme","HOXA and HOXD genes","Segment identity","Synpolydactyly (HOXD13); hand-foot-genital syndrome (HOXA13)"]],"caption":"Name the abnormal axis to find the responsible center and signal."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a chick embryo, the AER is removed once early in outgrowth and, in a second embryo, late in outgrowth. How will the two limbs differ?","explain":"Early removal leaves only proximal structures, because outgrowth stops before the forearm and hand are laid down. Late removal loses only the most distal elements, such as the digits, because proximal segments were already laid down.","check":"The AER paragraph and the proximal to distal sequence."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C04","obj":"MS-1.4","title":"Limb rotation, compartments, and dermatomes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both limb buds start with the same layout. The cranial border is the preaxial border, which will carry the radius and thumb in the arm and the tibia and big toe in the leg; the caudal border is postaxial. Myoblasts gather into a dorsal muscle mass, which forms the extensors and is supplied by posterior division nerves of the plexus, and a ventral mass, which forms the flexors (and thigh adductors) and is supplied by anterior division nerves. Dermatomes run in segmental order, cranial segments preaxial and caudal segments postaxial."},{"type":"p","text":"In weeks 7 and 8 the limbs bend at the elbow and knee and rotate about their long axes in opposite directions. The upper limb rotates about 90 degrees laterally, so the elbow points backward, the extensors lie posteriorly, and the thumb lies laterally. The lower limb rotates about 90 degrees medially, so the knee points forward, the extensors lie anteriorly, the flexors posteriorly, and the big toe medially. The palm and the sole are therefore both ventral, flexor surfaces. Its cellular cause is not fully understood, but the result suits each limb's job: lateral rotation turns the palms forward for grasping, and medial rotation puts the knee extensors in front to straighten and lock the knee for weight bearing, with the sole on the ground."},{"type":"p","text":"Because muscles and nerves keep their embryonic surface, rotation predicts compartment and nerve. Posterior division nerves (axillary and radial in the arm; femoral, superior and inferior gluteal, and common fibular in the leg) supply the original dorsal, extensor surface, which is now the back of the arm but the front of the thigh and leg. Anterior division nerves (musculocutaneous, median, and ulnar; obturator and tibial) supply the original ventral surface, now the front of the arm but the medial and posterior thigh and the back of the leg. Dorsiflexion of the ankle is an extensor action, so it is performed by anterior leg muscles."},{"type":"p","text":"Dermatomes are mapped before rotation, so rotation twists them. In the upper limb the pattern stays readable: C5 and C6 run along the lateral (preaxial) side to the thumb, C7 reaches the middle finger, and C8 and T1 run along the medial (postaxial) side. In the lower limb, medial rotation carries the preaxial strip around to the front and medial side, so the bands run obliquely downward and medially in a spiral: L1 near the inguinal ligament, L2 and L3 over the anterior thigh, L4 over the knee and medial leg, L5 over the lateral leg and dorsum of the foot, S1 over the lateral border and sole of the foot, and S2 up the back of the leg and thigh."},{"type":"table","title":"Opposite rotations of the upper and lower limb","cols":["Feature","Upper limb","Lower limb"],"rows":[["Rotation in weeks 7 to 8","About 90 degrees lateral","About 90 degrees medial"],["Preaxial digit","Thumb, lateral","Big toe, medial"],["Extensor (dorsal mass) compartment","Posterior","Anterior"],["Flexor (ventral mass) compartment","Anterior","Posterior (adductors medial)"],["Posterior division nerves","Axillary, radial","Femoral, gluteal, common fibular"],["Preaxial dermatomes","C5, C6 on the lateral side","L4 on the medial leg"]],"caption":"Find the embryonic surface first, then apply the rotation."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting compartment and nerve from rotation","text":"Where should quadriceps lie, and which nerve supplies it? It extends the knee, so it is a dorsal mass muscle supplied by a posterior division nerve: femoral, L2 to L4. Medial rotation carried the dorsal surface forward, so quadriceps lies anteriorly. Triceps is also dorsal mass and posterior division (radial), but lateral rotation left the dorsal surface behind, so it lies posteriorly. The hamstrings flex the knee, so they are ventral mass muscles supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve (anterior divisions) and lie posteriorly. The one exception, the short head of biceps femoris, is supplied by the common fibular division."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C05","obj":"MS-1.5","title":"Ossification and joint formation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone forms by two routes, and the route decides which bones a disorder affects. In intramembranous ossification, mesenchymal cells condense, switch on the transcription factor RUNX2, and differentiate directly into osteoblasts that secrete osteoid, which then mineralizes. Woven bone spicules join into trabeculae, later remodeled into compact plates. This route builds the flat bones of the skull vault, most facial bones, much of the mandible, and most of the clavicle, the first bone to begin ossifying at about weeks 5 to 6. Skull bones grow at their edges, so the sutures and fontanelles let the vault enlarge with the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"In endochondral ossification, mesenchyme condenses and, under SOX9, forms a hyaline cartilage model covered by perichondrium. A cartilage model can grow from within by chondrocyte division, which bone cannot, so long bones, vertebrae, ribs, the pelvis, and the skull base use this route. The primary ossification center forms in the diaphysis, and by about week 12 primary centers are present in all the long bones of the limbs. Secondary centers appear in the epiphyses, mostly after birth; the distal femoral center is usually present at term. Between them, the epiphyseal growth plate keeps producing cartilage that is replaced from the diaphyseal side, lengthening the bone until the plates close after puberty under the influence of estrogen."},{"type":"flow","title":"Endochondral ossification of a long bone","steps":["Mesenchyme forms a hyaline cartilage model with perichondrium","Midshaft perichondrium becomes periosteum and lays a bone collar","Central chondrocytes hypertrophy, calcify matrix, release VEGF, and die","Periosteal bud vessels bring osteoprogenitors and osteoclast precursors","Primary ossification center forms in the diaphysis","Secondary centers form in the epiphyses, mostly after birth","Growth plates lengthen the bone, then close after puberty"],"caption":"Cartilage is not turned into bone; calcified cartilage is removed and bone replaces it."},{"type":"p","text":"Growth plate chondrocytes are held in balance by a feedback loop in which Indian hedgehog from prehypertrophic chondrocytes stimulates PTHrP near the bone ends, and PTHrP keeps chondrocytes dividing and delays their hypertrophy. FGFR3 signaling restrains chondrocyte proliferation. A disorder acting mainly on one route shows mainly in its bones, which makes the skeletal pattern diagnostic (see the STOP below)."},{"type":"table","title":"Intramembranous versus endochondral ossification","cols":["Feature","Intramembranous","Endochondral"],"rows":[["Template","None; mesenchyme becomes bone directly","Hyaline cartilage model"],["Key transcription factor","RUNX2","SOX9 for cartilage, then RUNX2"],["Main bones","Skull vault, facial bones, most of clavicle","Long bones, vertebrae, ribs, pelvis, skull base"],["Growth in length","At edges (sutures)","At growth plates"],["Classic disorder","Cleidocranial dysplasia (RUNX2)","Achondroplasia (FGFR3)"]],"caption":"RUNX2 is needed for osteoblasts in both routes, but its loss shows most clearly in intramembranous bones."},{"type":"p","text":"Joints form at interzones, bands of dense mesenchyme between adjacent cartilage models, from about week 6, and by the end of week 8 they resemble adult joints. Interzone cells express GDF5 and make no cartilage. Where the interzone becomes dense fibrous tissue, a fibrous joint forms, such as a suture; where it becomes cartilage, a cartilaginous joint forms, such as the pubic symphysis. In a synovial joint the central interzone cavitates, through cell death and hyaluronan secretion, to form the joint cavity, while the surrounding mesenchyme forms the capsule, synovial membrane, and ligaments, and intra-articular structures such as menisci also arise from the interzone. Cavitation depends on fetal movement, so disorders that immobilize the fetus leave stiff joints at birth (arthrogryposis)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"One child has absent clavicles, a wide anterior fontanelle, and extra teeth. Another has short arms and legs, a large skull vault with a flat midface, and a near-normal trunk length. Which ossification route fails in each?","explain":"The first has cleidocranial dysplasia: loss of one RUNX2 copy impairs osteoblast formation, most visibly in intramembranous bones. The second has achondroplasia: gain-of-function FGFR3 slows growth plate proliferation, so endochondral long bones and skull base are short while the intramembranous vault grows normally.","check":"The ossification comparison table and the growth plate paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C06","obj":"MS-1.6","title":"Pharyngeal arch muscles and their nerves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pharyngeal arches are paired bars of mesenchyme that appear in weeks 4 and 5 on each side of the developing head and neck, separated externally by clefts and internally by pouches. Arches 1, 2, 3, 4, and 6 develop in humans; the fifth is absent or rudimentary. Each arch is covered by ectoderm outside and endoderm inside and contains four components: a skeletal element and connective tissue from neural crest, an aortic arch artery, muscle from cranial paraxial mesoderm, and a cranial nerve that grows in from the adjacent hindbrain."},{"type":"p","text":"Each arch's muscle is supplied by the nerve of its own arch and keeps that nerve wherever it migrates, just as somite muscle keeps its spinal nerve. Temporalis spreads up to the temple and the facial muscles spread over the scalp and down the neck as platysma, yet they remain supplied by the nerves of arches 1 and 2. The arch nerves run V3, VII, IX, X, so the motor nerve of a head or neck muscle follows from its arch (table). The fourth arch is supplied by the superior laryngeal and pharyngeal branches of the vagus, and the sixth by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. The right recurrent nerve hooks under the subclavian artery (fourth arch artery) and the left under the aortic arch at the ligamentum arteriosum (sixth arch artery), because the distal right sixth arch artery regresses."},{"type":"table","title":"Pharyngeal arch muscles, nerves, and lesion signs","cols":["Arch","Muscles","Nerve","Sign of nerve lesion"],"rows":[["1st","Masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini","CN V3 (mandibular)","Weak chewing; jaw deviates toward the lesion on opening"],["2nd","Muscles of facial expression, buccinator, platysma, stapedius, stylohyoid, posterior belly of digastric","CN VII (facial)","Facial paralysis; hyperacusis from stapedius loss"],["3rd","Stylopharyngeus","CN IX (glossopharyngeal)","Weak elevation of the pharynx, rarely detectable alone"],["4th","Pharyngeal constrictors, cricothyroid, levator veli palatini, palatoglossus","CN X (superior laryngeal and pharyngeal branches)","Dysphagia; uvula deviates away from the lesion; loss of high pitch with cricothyroid palsy"],["6th","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","CN X (recurrent laryngeal)","Hoarseness from vocal fold paralysis"]],"caption":"Arches 1, 2, and 3 use nerves V3, VII, and IX; arches 4 and 6 both use the vagus."},{"type":"p","text":"Lesion signs follow the muscle actions. With a V3 lesion, the intact lateral pterygoid pulls its condyle forward unopposed, so the jaw deviates toward the weak side. With a VII lesion, stapedius no longer damps ossicle movement, so sounds seem too loud. With a vagal lesion, the intact levator veli palatini pulls the uvula away from the lesion. Cricothyroid tilts the thyroid cartilage to lengthen and tense the vocal folds, so its loss lowers vocal pitch range, while the sixth arch muscles open, close, and adjust the folds, so recurrent nerve injury causes hoarseness."},{"type":"p","text":"Two exceptions are often tested. The tongue muscles, except palatoglossus, come from occipital somites and are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), not an arch nerve. Stylopharyngeus is the only third arch muscle and is a longitudinal elevator, whereas the constrictors belong to the fourth arch and the vagus."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a lesion with the arch map","text":"A man cannot close his left eye or raise the left corner of his mouth, and loud sounds hurt his left ear. Chewing and jaw opening are normal, and his uvula is midline. Facial expression muscles and stapedius are second arch muscles, so the lesion is the left facial nerve, CN VII, proximal to the branch to stapedius in the facial canal. Normal chewing and jaw opening exclude a first arch (V3) lesion, and a midline uvula excludes a vagal (fourth arch) lesion. The posterior belly of digastric and stylohyoid are also second arch muscles, but their weakness is rarely noticed clinically."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"One rule ties this chapter together: a muscle keeps the nerve of the segment or arch where it formed, however far it migrates. Somite muscle keeps its spinal nerve and divides into epaxial (dorsal ramus) and hypaxial (ventral ramus) territories; limb muscle keeps the posterior or anterior division nerve of its dorsal or ventral mass even after rotation turns that surface to the front or back; and arch muscle keeps V3, VII, IX, or X. Clinical dermatome and myotome maps, the anterior thigh extensors, the medial big toe, and the vagal supply of the larynx all follow from this rule plus the movements of development."},{"type":"p","text":"Molecular biology and pathology explain the defects. A single signal or gene acting at one step gives a predictable lesion: FGF from the AER for limb length, Shh from the ZPA for digit pattern, LMX1B for dorsal structures, HOXD13 and HOXA13 for distal identity, FGFR3 for growth plate proliferation, and RUNX2 for osteoblasts. Teratogens such as thalidomide act only during the window when their target step is underway. Three limits apply: timing figures are approximate and differ among texts, the progress zone model of limb outgrowth is a simplification still debated, and the cause of limb rotation is described better than it is explained."}],"summary":["Somites form from paraxial mesoderm from about day 20; Shh from notochord and floor plate makes sclerotome (vertebrae, ribs), and Wnt maintains dermomyotome (myotome and back dermis).","Epaxial myotome forms the deep back muscles supplied by dorsal rami; hypaxial myotome forms body wall and limb muscles supplied by ventral rami; limb bone and tendon come from lateral plate mesoderm.","Each vertebral body forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome and the cranial half of the next, so myotomes span discs, nerves exit at disc level, and there are eight cervical nerves but seven cervical vertebrae.","The notochord persists as the nucleus pulposus; its rests can form chordoma in the clivus or sacrum.","AER FGFs drive proximal to distal outgrowth; ZPA Shh sets digit identity, and ectopic anterior Shh causes preaxial polydactyly; WNT7A and LMX1B set the dorsal side; HOX genes set segment identity.","Thalidomide during limb outgrowth (about days 24 to 36) causes phocomelia.","The upper limb rotates laterally and the lower limb medially, so extensors are posterior in the arm and anterior in the thigh and leg, the thumb is lateral and the big toe medial, and lower limb dermatomes spiral.","Intramembranous ossification (skull vault, most of the clavicle) needs RUNX2 and no cartilage; endochondral ossification replaces a hyaline cartilage model and lengthens bone at growth plates.","Joints form from interzone mesenchyme; synovial cavities form by cavitation, which requires fetal movement.","Arch muscles keep their nerves: first V3 (mastication), second VII (facial expression), third IX (stylopharyngeus), fourth and sixth X (pharynx and larynx)."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Axial Skeleton; Muscular System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Limbs; Head and Neck (pharyngeal arches)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back; Upper Limb; Lower Limb (dermatomes and nerve supply of compartments)"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone (osteogenesis and bone growth)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"}]},"MS-2":{"unit":"MS-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01","title":"Muscle, Bone, and Joint Histology","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the microscopic structure of the musculoskeletal system: the sarcomere, the three muscle types, compact and spongy bone, the cells that build and remove bone, the epiphyseal growth plate, and the three classes of joints. Board questions rarely ask for a label alone. They ask you to predict what changes when a filament slides, a receptor is overactive, a mineral is missing, or a tissue has no blood supply. Each structure here is explained by the job it does, so that achondroplasia, rickets, osteopetrosis, and cartilage that heals poorly follow directly from normal histology.","prerequisites":["Basic tissue types and the structure of collagen and proteoglycans","Cell junctions: gap junctions, desmosomes, and adherens junctions","Calcium and phosphate balance with PTH and vitamin D (EN-5)","Endochondral and intramembranous ossification (MS-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Muscle contraction (MS-7) and cardiac muscle contraction (CV-5)","how":"The sarcomere, triads, and dyads described here are the structures that excitation-contraction coupling uses."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal connective tissue (MS-6)","how":"Type I collagen in bone and ligament and type II collagen with aggrecan in cartilage explain the different strengths of each tissue."},{"to":"Endocrine function and control (EN-5)","how":"PTH, calcitriol, FGF23, estrogen, and growth hormone act on the bone cells and growth plate chondrocytes described in this chapter."},{"to":"Metabolic and congenital musculoskeletal disease (MS-13-C, MS-13-D)","how":"Osteoporosis, osteopetrosis, rickets, and achondroplasia are each a defect in one cell or one zone named here."},{"to":"Joints: structure and function (MS-4)","how":"Joint class sets how much a joint moves and how its cartilage is fed, which shapes how arthritis develops."}],"terms":[{"term":"Sarcomere","def":"The contractile unit of striated muscle, extending from one Z line to the next."},{"term":"Triad","def":"A T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of sarcoplasmic reticulum, found at the A band to I band junction in skeletal muscle."},{"term":"Intercalated disc","def":"The step-shaped junction between cardiac muscle cells that contains fascia adherens, desmosomes, and gap junctions."},{"term":"Dense body","def":"A cytoplasmic or membrane-attached plaque rich in alpha-actinin that anchors thin filaments in smooth muscle, the functional equivalent of a Z line."},{"term":"Osteon","def":"The structural unit of compact bone: concentric lamellae arranged around a central (Haversian) canal."},{"term":"Osteoid","def":"Newly secreted, unmineralized bone matrix, mostly type I collagen."},{"term":"RANKL","def":"A ligand on osteoblast-lineage cells that binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their formation and activation."},{"term":"Osteoprotegerin (OPG)","def":"A soluble decoy receptor made by osteoblasts that binds RANKL and prevents osteoclast activation."},{"term":"Howship lacuna","def":"The shallow pit on a bone surface that an active osteoclast excavates beneath its ruffled border."},{"term":"Growth plate (physis)","def":"The disc of hyaline cartilage between epiphysis and metaphysis whose chondrocytes drive lengthening of long bones."},{"term":"Synchondrosis","def":"A primary cartilaginous joint in which bones are joined by hyaline cartilage."},{"term":"Symphysis","def":"A secondary cartilaginous joint in which bones are joined by fibrocartilage, usually in the midline."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C01","obj":"MS-2.1","title":"The sarcomere and its bands","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle look striated because their myofibrils are built of repeating sarcomeres lined up in register across the cell. A sarcomere runs from one Z line (Z disc) to the next. Thin filaments of actin, with tropomyosin and troponin, are anchored at the Z line by alpha-actinin and project toward the center. Thick filaments of myosin sit in the center, held in place at the M line and tethered to the Z line by the giant elastic protein titin."},{"type":"p","text":"The bands are defined by which filaments are present. The A band (anisotropic: birefringent in polarized light, dark in stained sections) is the full length of the thick filaments, including the parts that overlap thin filaments. The I band (isotropic, light) contains thin filaments only and is bisected by the Z line, so each I band belongs to two neighboring sarcomeres. The H zone is the central part of the A band where thick filaments are not overlapped by thin filaments, and the M line runs through its middle, where proteins such as myomesin cross-link the thick filaments."},{"type":"table","title":"Sarcomere landmarks and what happens to them","cols":["Landmark","Contains or defined by","During contraction"],"rows":[["Z line","Alpha-actinin anchoring thin filaments; sarcomere boundary","Z lines move closer together"],["I band","Thin filaments only","Shortens"],["A band","Whole length of thick filaments","Stays constant"],["H zone","Thick filaments only, no overlap","Shortens, may disappear"],["M line","Cross-linking proteins in the center of the thick filaments","Stays at the center"],["Sarcomere (Z to Z)","One contractile unit","Shortens"]],"caption":"Only regions defined by the absence of overlap change length; the A band is set by the length of the thick filament itself."},{"type":"p","text":"These changes follow from the sliding filament mechanism. Neither actin nor myosin filaments change length. Myosin heads on each half of the bipolar thick filament pull their thin filaments toward the M line, so overlap increases. The non-overlap regions (I band and H zone) therefore narrow, the Z lines approach each other, and the A band is unchanged. When a muscle is passively stretched the opposite occurs: the I band and H zone widen while the A band still stays constant."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating band widths","text":"Take thick filaments of about 1.6 micrometers and thin filaments of about 1.0 micrometer extending from each Z line, ignoring Z line thickness. At a sarcomere length of 2.4 micrometers, the A band is 1.6, the I band is 2.4 minus 1.6, or 0.8, and the H zone is 2.4 minus 2 times 1.0, or 0.4. If the sarcomere shortens to 2.0 micrometers, the A band is still 1.6, the I band falls to 0.4, and the H zone falls to 0. The I band and H zone each lost 0.4 micrometers, exactly the amount the sarcomere shortened, because the thin filaments slid that distance."},{"type":"p","text":"The amount of overlap also sets force. Near the length where every myosin head can reach actin and thin filaments do not collide, tension is maximal; at very long lengths few cross-bridges form, and at very short lengths filaments collide and interfere. This length-tension relationship (MS-7) is the sarcomere basis of the Frank-Starling mechanism in the heart."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A muscle fiber is stretched passively well beyond its resting length. Which bands change, and in which direction?","explain":"The I band and H zone widen because the thin filaments are pulled out of overlap, and the Z lines move apart. The A band does not change because thick filament length does not change.","check":"The landmark table and the sliding filament paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C02","obj":"MS-2.2","title":"Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle on histology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each muscle type is built around how it receives its signal to contract and how its cells work together. Recognizing them on a slide comes down to four questions: is the tissue striated, where are the nuclei, how many are there, and how are the cells joined."},{"type":"p","text":"A skeletal muscle fiber is a single huge cell formed by fusion of myoblasts, so it is multinucleate, with many nuclei pressed flat against the sarcolemma. In cross section fibers are polygonal with peripheral nuclei. Because each fiber is wide and must contract uniformly along its length, T tubules (inward extensions of the sarcolemma) dive into the fiber at every A band to I band junction and pair with two terminal cisternae of sarcoplasmic reticulum to form triads, giving rapid, uniform calcium release. Mature fiber nuclei do not divide; repair depends on satellite cells lying between the sarcolemma and the basal lamina. Centrally placed nuclei in skeletal muscle are therefore abnormal and indicate regeneration or a myopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac muscle cells are separate cells that must contract as one unit. They are branched, with one or occasionally two central nuclei, often surrounded by a pale perinuclear zone that collects lipofuscin with age. Intercalated discs join cells end to end. Their transverse portions carry fascia adherens, which anchor actin filaments, and desmosomes, which link desmin intermediate filaments and keep cells from pulling apart; their lateral portions carry gap junctions (mainly connexin 43) that let depolarization spread from cell to cell so the myocardium behaves as a functional syncytium. Cardiac T tubules are wider and lie at the Z lines, usually pairing with a single SR cisterna as a dyad, which fits the cardiac reliance on calcium entering through L-type channels to trigger SR calcium release. Adult cardiomyocytes have very limited capacity to divide, so injury heals by fibrous scar and increased workload causes hypertrophy."},{"type":"p","text":"Smooth muscle cells are fusiform with one central, cigar-shaped nucleus that may look corkscrewed in a contracted cell. Actin and myosin are not arranged in register, so there are no striations; thin filaments attach to dense bodies in the cytoplasm and on the membrane, and desmin intermediate filaments link the dense bodies into a network that pulls the cell into a shorter, rounder shape. There are no T tubules; caveolae and a sparse SR serve the small cell. Single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle is coupled by gap junctions. Smooth muscle can divide, so organs such as the uterus in pregnancy enlarge by both hypertrophy and hyperplasia."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the three muscle types","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Shape","Long cylindrical fiber","Branched cell","Fusiform (spindle)"],["Nuclei","Many, peripheral","One or two, central","One, central"],["Striations","Yes","Yes","No"],["Junctions between cells","None (each fiber innervated)","Intercalated discs: fascia adherens, desmosomes, gap junctions","Gap junctions in single-unit muscle"],["T tubule arrangement","Triads at A to I junction","Dyads at Z line, wider T tubules","No T tubules; caveolae"],["Thin filament anchor","Z line","Z line","Dense bodies"],["Response to injury or load","Satellite cell regeneration; hypertrophy","Scar; hypertrophy","Hyperplasia and hypertrophy"]],"caption":"Peripheral nuclei plus striations means skeletal; central nuclei plus striations and intercalated discs means cardiac; central nucleus without striations means smooth."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C03","obj":"MS-2.3","title":"Compact bone, spongy bone, and woven versus lamellar bone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone matrix is type I collagen hardened by hydroxyapatite crystals. Mineral gives stiffness and collagen gives resistance to fracture, but mineralized matrix blocks diffusion, so every osteocyte must stay within a short distance of a blood vessel. The architecture of bone is largely the solution to that supply problem."},{"type":"p","text":"Compact (cortical) bone forms the shafts of long bones and the outer shell of all bones. Its unit is the osteon (Haversian system), a cylinder of concentric lamellae running along the long axis of the bone around a central Haversian canal that carries capillaries and nerves. Collagen fibers in adjacent lamellae run in alternating directions, which resists loads from several directions. Osteocytes sit in lacunae between lamellae and extend processes through tiny channels called canaliculi; the processes meet through gap junctions, so nutrients and signals pass from the central canal outward cell by cell. Volkmann (perforating) canals run across the bone, roughly perpendicular to osteons, and connect Haversian canals to each other, to the periosteum, and to the marrow cavity; they are not surrounded by concentric lamellae. Each osteon is bounded by a cement line. Between osteons lie interstitial lamellae, the remnants of older, partly resorbed osteons, and circumferential lamellae line the outer and inner surfaces."},{"type":"p","text":"The periosteum covers the outer surface. Its outer fibrous layer is anchored by collagen (Sharpey) fibers, and its inner layer holds osteoprogenitor cells that allow growth in width and fracture repair. A thinner endosteum lines the marrow cavity and trabeculae."},{"type":"p","text":"Spongy (cancellous, trabecular) bone, found in vertebral bodies, the ends of long bones, and the interior of flat bones, is a lattice of thin plates and struts called trabeculae with marrow in the spaces between them. Trabeculae are thin enough to be nourished from the marrow surface, so they lack osteons and Haversian canals. Their large surface area makes trabecular bone turn over faster than cortical bone, which is why trabecular-rich sites such as vertebrae, the distal radius, and the femoral neck lose mass early in osteoporosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Woven versus lamellar bone","cols":["Feature","Woven (primary) bone","Lamellar (secondary) bone"],"rows":[["Collagen arrangement","Random, interlacing","Parallel sheets in ordered lamellae"],["Osteocytes","Many, large, irregular","Fewer, evenly spaced"],["Speed of formation","Fast","Slow"],["Strength","Weak","Strong"],["Normal setting","Fetal skeleton, early fracture callus","Nearly all of the adult skeleton"],["Abnormal setting in adults","Paget disease, fibrous dysplasia, bone tumors, severe hyperparathyroidism","Not applicable"]],"caption":"Woven bone trades strength for speed and is normally remodeled into lamellar bone; its presence in adult bone signals rapid abnormal formation."},{"type":"p","text":"Paget disease shows why the distinction matters. Disordered, excessive osteoclast resorption followed by disorganized osteoblast repair forms woven bone early and later a mosaic of lamellar bone with irregular cement lines, so the bone is thicker yet weaker and prone to bowing and fracture."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C04","obj":"MS-2.4","title":"Osteoblasts, osteocytes, and osteoclasts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone is remodeled throughout life. Formation and resorption are coupled because the osteoblast lineage controls the osteoclast, and most hormones that change bone mass act at that point."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteoblasts arise from mesenchymal stem cells under the transcription factors RUNX2 and osterix, with Wnt and beta-catenin signaling promoting their differentiation. They are cuboidal cells with basophilic cytoplasm, rich in rough ER, lining bone surfaces. They secrete osteoid, which is mostly type I collagen with noncollagenous proteins such as osteocalcin, and they express alkaline phosphatase, which raises local phosphate and breaks down pyrophosphate, an inhibitor of mineralization, so hydroxyapatite can form in the osteoid. Serum bone-specific alkaline phosphatase therefore rises when bone formation is active, as in growth, fracture healing, Paget disease, and rickets. Osteoblasts carry the PTH receptor and vitamin D receptor and secrete both RANKL and OPG."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteocytes are osteoblasts walled in by their own matrix; they are the most numerous bone cells and connect through canaliculi. Fluid flow in the canaliculi during loading lets them act as mechanosensors. Unloading increases their secretion of sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt signaling in osteoblasts and reduces formation, while loading suppresses sclerostin. Osteocytes are also the main source of FGF23, which lowers serum phosphate (see the cycle below)."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteoclasts come from the monocyte and macrophage lineage, not from mesenchyme. M-CSF and RANKL drive precursors to fuse into large multinucleated cells with acidophilic cytoplasm. An active osteoclast seals onto bone with integrin alpha-v beta-3 at a ring-shaped sealing zone. Inside the ring, its ruffled border pumps H+ through a vacuolar H+ ATPase, with H+ supplied by carbonic anhydrase II and chloride following through the ClC-7 channel; the resulting acid dissolves hydroxyapatite, and the protease cathepsin K then digests the exposed collagen. The pit left behind is a Howship lacuna. Osteoclasts express tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP) and calcitonin receptors."},{"type":"flow","title":"Coupled remodeling","steps":["PTH or local signals raise RANKL relative to OPG","RANKL binds RANK on monocyte-lineage precursors (with M-CSF)","Precursors fuse into multinucleated osteoclasts","Ruffled border releases acid and cathepsin K","Howship lacuna forms; matrix factors recruit osteoblasts","Osteoblasts lay osteoid that then mineralizes","Some osteoblasts are trapped and become osteocytes"],"caption":"PTH acts on osteoblast-lineage cells, which signal osteoclast precursors through RANKL; osteoclasts lack PTH receptors."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Osteocyte FGF23 and phosphate","steps":["Serum phosphate or calcitriol rises","Osteocytes increase FGF23 secretion","Kidney reduces proximal phosphate reabsorption","FGF23 also lowers 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so calcitriol falls","Serum phosphate falls, reducing FGF23 drive"],"caption":"Excess FGF23, as in X-linked hypophosphatemia from PHEX mutation, causes renal phosphate wasting and rickets or osteomalacia."},{"type":"p","text":"This control explains several classic findings. Continuously high PTH raises RANKL relative to OPG and causes net resorption, whereas estrogen restrains RANKL signaling and shortens osteoclast life, so its loss at menopause speeds resorption. If osteoclasts cannot acidify their lacuna, bone is not removed, producing osteopetrosis: dense, brittle bone that crowds the marrow and narrows cranial foramina. Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency adds renal tubular acidosis, and cathepsin K deficiency causes pycnodysostosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A mouse lacks the gene for osteoprotegerin. What happens to its bone mass, and why?","explain":"Bone mass falls. Without the decoy receptor, RANKL binds RANK unopposed, so osteoclast formation and resorption outpace formation.","check":"The RANKL and OPG steps in the remodeling flow."},{"type":"example","title":"Dense bones that break","text":"A child has fractures after minor falls, anemia, metabolic acidosis with an inappropriately high urine pH, and uniformly dense bones on radiographs. Dense bone means bone is made but not removed, so osteoclasts are failing. The marrow cavity shrinks, causing anemia, and old microdamaged matrix is never replaced, so bone is dense but brittle. A defect shared by bone and renal tubules points to the enzyme both use to generate secreted H+: carbonic anhydrase II. The diagnosis is osteopetrosis with renal tubular acidosis from carbonic anhydrase II deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C05","obj":"MS-2.5","title":"The epiphyseal growth plate, achondroplasia, and rickets","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Long bones lengthen by endochondral ossification at the growth plate (physis), a disc of hyaline cartilage between the epiphysis and the metaphysis. Bone does not grow by expansion of existing bone; cartilage is added on the epiphyseal side and replaced by bone on the metaphyseal side. The plate keeps a fairly constant thickness during growth because the two processes run at matched rates."},{"type":"flow","title":"Growth plate zones, epiphysis to diaphysis","steps":["Reserve zone: scattered resting chondrocytes supply new cells","Proliferative zone: chondrocytes divide into stacked columns","Hypertrophic zone: cells swell, make type X collagen and VEGF","Calcification zone: matrix mineralizes, chondrocytes die","Ossification zone: vessels invade, osteoblasts lay bone on cartilage"],"caption":"Proliferation and hypertrophy produce length; mineralization of cartilage and vascular invasion let bone replace it."},{"type":"p","text":"Length comes from two steps: division in the proliferative zone and enlargement of chondrocytes in the hypertrophic zone, which accounts for much of the gain. Hypertrophic chondrocytes release VEGF and matrix vesicles; once their matrix calcifies they die, and the calcified cartilage becomes a scaffold. Capillaries and osteoprogenitor cells from the marrow invade, and osteoblasts deposit bone on the calcified cartilage, producing mixed spicules with a cartilage core and a bone covering that are later remodeled. Proliferation is regulated by growth hormone through IGF-1, thyroid hormone, a local Indian hedgehog and PTHrP feedback loop, and FGFR3 signaling, which acts as a brake. Estrogen in both sexes eventually closes the plate. The hypertrophic zone, with large cells and little matrix, is the mechanically weakest layer, so physeal fractures and slipped capital femoral epiphysis usually pass through it."},{"type":"p","text":"Achondroplasia is a gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3, autosomal dominant but usually a new mutation, often linked to advanced paternal age. The overactive receptor suppresses chondrocyte proliferation and hypertrophy, so the proliferative zone is thin with short, disorganized columns. Bones formed by endochondral ossification are short, especially the proximal limb segments, while bones formed by intramembranous ossification, such as the skull vault, grow normally. The result is short limbs with a near-normal trunk, a relatively large head with frontal bossing, and a small cranial base that can narrow the foramen magnum."},{"type":"p","text":"Rickets is defective mineralization of the growing skeleton, from vitamin D deficiency or from phosphate loss. Low phosphate prevents the normal apoptosis of hypertrophic chondrocytes, and unmineralized cartilage cannot be invaded by vessels and replaced. Hypertrophic chondrocytes therefore pile up in long, disordered columns, the plate becomes wide and irregular, and osteoid accumulates without mineral. Radiographs show widened plates with frayed, cupped metaphyses. Clinically, enlarged costochondral junctions form the rachitic rosary, and soft bones bow under weight bearing. In adults, whose plates have closed, the same mineral defect causes osteomalacia, with excess osteoid on bone surfaces but no plate changes."},{"type":"table","title":"Growth plate in achondroplasia and rickets","cols":["Feature","Achondroplasia","Rickets"],"rows":[["Primary defect","Too little chondrocyte proliferation (FGFR3 activation)","Too little mineralization (low vitamin D or phosphate)"],["Zone most affected","Proliferative zone thin","Hypertrophic zone expanded and disordered"],["Plate width","Thin","Wide and irregular"],["Bone mineral","Normal","Low, with excess osteoid"],["Skull vault","Normal size (intramembranous)","Soft (craniotabes), delayed fontanelle closure"],["Typical serum alkaline phosphatase","Normal","Elevated"]],"caption":"Achondroplasia makes too little cartilage; rickets makes cartilage that cannot be converted to bone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C06","obj":"MS-2.6","title":"Fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial joints","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Joints are classified by the tissue that binds the bones together, and that tissue determines how much movement is possible. Fibrous joints are joined by dense connective tissue and move little, cartilaginous joints by cartilage and move slightly, and synovial joints have a fluid-filled cavity and move freely."},{"type":"table","title":"Joint classes","cols":["Class and subtype","Binding tissue","Examples","Notes"],"rows":[["Fibrous: suture","Thin dense connective tissue","Sutures of the skull vault","Allow molding at birth and skull growth; may fuse with age"],["Fibrous: syndesmosis","Ligament or interosseous membrane","Distal tibiofibular joint; radius and ulna interosseous membrane","Slight movement"],["Fibrous: gomphosis","Periodontal ligament","Tooth in its alveolar socket","Peg in socket"],["Cartilaginous: synchondrosis (primary)","Hyaline cartilage","Epiphyseal plate; spheno-occipital; first sternocostal joint","Most are temporary and ossify"],["Cartilaginous: symphysis (secondary)","Fibrocartilage, with thin hyaline cartilage on the bone ends","Pubic symphysis; intervertebral disc; manubriosternal joint","Midline, slight movement, permanent"],["Synovial","Joint capsule enclosing a synovial cavity","Knee, hip, shoulder","Free movement; articular hyaline cartilage"]],"caption":"Synchondroses use hyaline cartilage and symphyses use fibrocartilage."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibrocartilage combines rows of chondrocytes with dense bundles of type I collagen in addition to type II, so it resists both compression and tension, suiting it to the pubic symphysis and the annulus fibrosus of the intervertebral disc. The disc's central nucleus pulposus is a gel derived from the notochord. When the annulus weakens, usually posterolaterally where the posterior longitudinal ligament is narrow, the nucleus can herniate and compress a nerve root."},{"type":"p","text":"A synovial joint is enclosed by a capsule with an outer fibrous layer and an inner synovial membrane. The synovium has no true epithelium and no basement membrane: one to a few layers of synoviocytes rest on vascular loose connective tissue. Type A synoviocytes are macrophage-like cells derived from bone marrow that clear debris; type B synoviocytes are fibroblast-like cells that secrete hyaluronan and lubricin. Synovial fluid is a filtrate of plasma enriched with these molecules. Hyaluronan makes the fluid viscous, and lubricin coats the cartilage surface to reduce friction."},{"type":"p","text":"Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage rich in type II collagen and aggrecan, whose negatively charged glycosaminoglycans hold water and resist compression. Unlike most hyaline cartilage it has no perichondrium, and it has no blood vessels, lymphatics, or nerves. Chondrocytes receive nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid, helped by the pumping effect of cyclic loading. With no vessels to bring repair cells and no perichondrium to supply new chondroblasts, damaged articular cartilage repairs poorly, which underlies osteoarthritis. In rheumatoid arthritis, by contrast, the disease begins in the vascular synovium, which proliferates into a pannus that erodes cartilage and bone."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A full-thickness defect in articular cartilage reaches the subchondral bone. How will it heal compared with a defect confined to the cartilage?","explain":"A defect limited to cartilage barely heals because no vessels or perichondrium can supply repair cells. A defect reaching subchondral bone lets marrow cells and blood enter, but they form fibrocartilage, which is weaker and wears faster than hyaline cartilage.","check":"The paragraph on articular cartilage nutrition and repair."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and histology set the pattern. Endochondral bones grow from a cartilage model at growth plates while intramembranous bones form directly in mesenchyme, which is why FGFR3 mutations shorten limbs but spare the skull vault. Muscle structure mirrors its signaling: triads, dyads, and caveolae match how each muscle type raises cytosolic calcium (MS-7, CV-5), and the ability to divide (smooth muscle), regenerate from satellite cells (skeletal), or only scar (cardiac) predicts the outcome of injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, biochemistry, and pathology meet at the bone cell. Bone mass reflects the balance of RANKL and OPG on osteoblast-lineage cells, with PTH, estrogen, and mechanical load acting there; mineralization depends on vitamin D, phosphate, FGF23, and alkaline phosphatase; and collagen types I and II determine the behavior of bone and cartilage. Histology explains appearance and mechanism but not every clinical detail, because the same pattern can have several causes: a wide growth plate is rickets from vitamin D deficiency or from renal phosphate wasting, and only serum chemistry separates them."}],"summary":["During contraction the I band, H zone, and sarcomere shorten while the A band, set by thick filament length, stays constant; no filament changes length.","Skeletal muscle is multinucleate with peripheral nuclei and triads; cardiac muscle is branched with central nuclei, dyads, and intercalated discs; smooth muscle is fusiform, nonstriated, and anchors actin to dense bodies.","Intercalated disc gap junctions make the myocardium a functional syncytium, while fascia adherens and desmosomes hold cells together.","Compact bone osteons keep osteocytes near Haversian vessels through canaliculi and gap junctions; Volkmann canals cross between osteons.","Spongy bone has no osteons and a large surface area, so it turns over faster and is lost first in osteoporosis; woven bone in adults signals abnormal rapid formation.","Osteoblasts (mesenchymal) make type I collagen osteoid and alkaline phosphatase and control osteoclasts through RANKL and OPG; osteocytes sense load and secrete sclerostin and FGF23.","Osteoclasts (monocyte lineage) dissolve mineral with acid from carbonic anhydrase II and digest collagen with cathepsin K in Howship lacunae; failure causes osteopetrosis.","Growth plate zones run reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, ossification; achondroplasia thins the proliferative zone and rickets widens a disordered hypertrophic zone.","Fibrous joints are sutures, syndesmoses, and gomphoses; synchondroses are hyaline and symphyses are fibrocartilage.","Articular cartilage lacks vessels and perichondrium and is fed by synovial fluid, whose hyaluronan and lubricin come from type B synoviocytes, so it repairs poorly."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Cartilage"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contraction of Skeletal Muscle"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Overview and basic concepts, joints"}]},"MS-3":{"unit":"MS-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01","title":"Axial and Appendicular Skeleton","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the bones of the spine, skull, and limbs and explains how their shapes follow from the work each region does. It relates regional vertebral features to movement and load, the normal spinal curves to their abnormal forms and to breathing, the skull openings to the nerves and vessels that pass through them, and the limb bones to the nerves and arteries that lie against them. Board questions usually describe a fracture, a curve, or a cranial nerve deficit and ask which structure is involved or what function is lost. If you know where each nerve touches bone and why each region is shaped as it is, most of these questions can be reasoned out rather than memorized.","prerequisites":["Bone structure, ossification, and joint types (MS-1, MS-2)","Cranial nerve functions and the trigeminal divisions (NE-8)","Brachial and lumbosacral plexus branches and the muscles they supply (NE-8, MS-5)","Meningeal layers and the epidural and subdural spaces (NE-4)","Lung volumes and spirometry (PU-6)"],"connections":[{"to":"Joints and muscles (MS-4, MS-5)","how":"The bony features in this chapter are the attachment sites and articular surfaces that set each joint's range and each muscle's action."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal trauma and degeneration (MS-13-H, MS-13-E)","how":"Fracture sites, osteoporotic vertebral collapse, and avascular necrosis follow directly from bone shape and blood supply."},{"to":"Cranial nerves and CNS trauma (NE-8, NE-20-H)","how":"Skull foramina explain grouped cranial nerve palsies, and the pterion explains the epidural hematoma."},{"to":"Peripheral nerve injury (NE-20-I)","how":"The axillary, radial, ulnar, and common fibular nerves are injured where they lie directly on bone."},{"to":"Restrictive lung disease (PU-14-A)","how":"Severe thoracic scoliosis is a chest wall cause of restriction that can progress to hypoventilation and cor pulmonale."}],"terms":[{"term":"Transverse foramen","def":"An opening in each cervical transverse process; those of C6 to C1 normally carry the vertebral artery."},{"term":"Dens","def":"The upward peg of the axis (C2), developmentally the body of C1, around which the atlas rotates."},{"term":"Costal facet","def":"An articular surface on a thoracic vertebral body or transverse process that receives the head or tubercle of a rib."},{"term":"Lordosis","def":"A sagittal curve of the spine that is concave posteriorly, normal in the cervical and lumbar regions."},{"term":"Kyphosis","def":"A sagittal curve of the spine that is concave anteriorly, normal in the thoracic and sacral regions."},{"term":"Scoliosis","def":"Lateral curvature of the spine of at least 10 degrees combined with rotation of the vertebrae."},{"term":"Cobb angle","def":"The angle between the end plates of the most tilted vertebrae at the top and bottom of a curve, used to measure its size."},{"term":"Pterion","def":"The H-shaped junction of the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones on the side of the skull, overlying the middle meningeal artery."},{"term":"Epidural hematoma","def":"Blood collected between the skull and the dura, usually arterial and usually from the middle meningeal artery."},{"term":"Surgical neck of the humerus","def":"The narrow region below the greater and lesser tubercles, a common fracture site where the axillary nerve winds around the bone."},{"term":"Avascular necrosis","def":"Death of bone after its blood supply is cut off, typical of the proximal scaphoid and the femoral head after fracture."},{"term":"Pes planus","def":"Flat foot, a low or collapsed medial longitudinal arch."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C01","obj":"MS-3.1","title":"Regional features of the vertebrae and what each region does","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A typical vertebra has a body in front for weight bearing and a vertebral arch behind (pedicles and laminae) enclosing the vertebral foramen, with transverse and spinous processes as muscle levers and paired articular processes forming the facet (zygapophyseal) joints. Body size, which rises with load, and facet orientation best explain what each region can do."},{"type":"p","text":"Cervical vertebrae carry only the head, so their bodies are small, and their oblique facets allow the widest range of flexion, extension, lateral flexion, and rotation. Each transverse process has a transverse foramen. The vertebral artery, the first branch of the subclavian artery, usually enters at C6 and rises through the foramina of C6 to C1 before entering the skull through the foramen magnum; C7's foramen usually carries only small veins. The bony canal shields the artery as the neck moves, but cervical fractures can tear or dissect it, causing brainstem or cerebellar stroke. C3 to C6 have short, usually bifid spines that anchor the ligamentum nuchae, and C7 (vertebra prominens) has the long single spine felt at the base of the neck."},{"type":"p","text":"The atlas (C1) is a ring of anterior and posterior arches and two lateral masses with no body and no spinous process; its superior facets cradle the occipital condyles, and the atlanto-occipital joints allow nodding. During development the body that would have belonged to C1 joins C2 as the dens, a peg that projects into the ring of the atlas. The atlas pivots around the dens at the atlantoaxial joints, which supply about half of cervical rotation. The transverse ligament of the atlas holds the dens against the anterior arch; if rheumatoid arthritis erodes it, ligament laxity in Down syndrome weakens it, or the dens fractures, C1 can slip forward on C2 and the dens can compress the upper cord."},{"type":"p","text":"Thoracic vertebrae carry the ribs. A typical rib head articulates with two adjacent bodies, so typical thoracic bodies have upper and lower costal facets (demifacets), and the transverse processes of T1 to T10 have facets for the rib tubercles. Near-coronal facets allow rotation, but the rib cage splints the region and the long, overlapping spines limit extension. Lumbar vertebrae carry the whole upper body, so they have the largest, kidney-shaped bodies and no transverse foramina or costal facets. Their curved, near-sagittal facets interlock, allowing flexion and extension but nearly blocking rotation. Because the cord ends at about L1 to L2 in adults, the lumbar canal holds the cauda equina, and the heavily loaded L4 to L5 and L5 to S1 discs are the commonest levels of herniation."},{"type":"table","title":"Regional vertebral features and their functional results","cols":["Region","Distinctive features","Facets and motion","Function served"],"rows":[["Cervical (C3 to C7)","Small bodies; transverse foramina; bifid spines C3 to C6","Oblique; widest range","Head mobility; vertebral artery protected"],["Atlas (C1)","No body or spinous process; arches and lateral masses","Atlanto-occipital: flexion and extension (nodding)","Carries the skull"],["Axis (C2)","Dens, held by the transverse ligament","Atlantoaxial: rotation","Pivot for turning the head"],["Thoracic","Costal facets on bodies and T1 to T10 transverse processes","Near coronal; rotation, little extension","Rib attachment; stable thoracic cage"],["Lumbar","Largest bodies; no transverse foramina or costal facets","Near sagittal; flexion and extension, little rotation","Weight bearing"]],"caption":"Body size rises with load; facet orientation sets which movements each region allows."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis develops neck pain, brisk reflexes, and weakness in all four limbs. Which structure has failed?","explain":"Synovitis has eroded the transverse ligament of the atlas, so C1 slides forward on C2 and the dens compresses the upper cervical cord, giving upper motor neuron signs in all four limbs.","check":"The atlas, dens, and transverse ligament paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C02","obj":"MS-3.2","title":"Normal spinal curves, scoliosis, and hyperkyphosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Seen from the side, the adult column has four curves: cervical lordosis, thoracic kyphosis, lumbar lordosis, and sacral kyphosis. The kyphoses are primary curves, the remnants of the flexed C-shape of the fetus, and come mainly from the shape of the vertebral bodies. The lordoses are secondary curves that come mainly from wedge-shaped discs; they begin before birth and become obvious when the infant holds up its head (cervical) and when the child sits, stands, and walks (lumbar). The alternating curves absorb axial shock like a spring, and the lordoses keep the center of gravity over the hips so standing needs little muscle work."},{"type":"p","text":"Excessive thoracic kyphosis (hyperkyphosis) in older adults most often follows osteoporotic compression fractures. Vertebral bodies are mostly trabecular bone, which osteoporosis thins early, and under flexion load the front of the body collapses into a wedge, so height falls and the back rounds. In adolescents, wedging of several adjacent vertebrae gives a rigid kyphosis (Scheuermann disease), and spinal tuberculosis destroys vertebral bodies to give a sharp angular gibbus. Excessive lumbar lordosis follows a forward shift of the center of gravity, as in late pregnancy."},{"type":"p","text":"Scoliosis is a lateral curve of at least 10 degrees (Cobb angle) combined with vertebral rotation, so it is a three-dimensional deformity, not simple side bending. The bodies rotate toward the convex side and the spinous processes toward the concavity, and the attached ribs are pushed backward on the convex side, producing a rib hump when the patient bends forward. A structural curve has fixed rotation and persists on bending; a functional curve from a short leg or muscle spasm has no fixed rotation and corrects when the cause is removed. About 80% of structural cases are idiopathic, mostly in adolescents, and progressive curves are far more common in girls. Other causes are hemivertebrae, neuromuscular disease (cerebral palsy, Duchenne muscular dystrophy), and Marfan syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Severe thoracic scoliosis restricts the lungs from outside. The rotated ribs crowd together on the concave side, the chest wall stiffens (low compliance), and the inspiratory muscles work at a mechanical disadvantage. The lungs and airways themselves are normal, so total lung capacity, vital capacity, and FVC fall while FEV1 falls in proportion, leaving FEV1/FVC normal or high. Function is usually clearly impaired once a thoracic curve passes about 70 degrees. Patients take small, rapid breaths, which raises the dead space fraction; alveolar hypoventilation, worst in sleep, causes hypercapnia and hypoxemia, and chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction leads to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale. Curves beginning before about age 5 also limit alveolar growth."},{"type":"table","title":"Normal and abnormal spinal curves","cols":["Curve","Plane","Normal or abnormal","Mechanism or typical cause"],"rows":[["Thoracic and sacral kyphosis","Sagittal, concave anteriorly","Normal primary","Fetal flexion; vertebral body shape"],["Cervical and lumbar lordosis","Sagittal, concave posteriorly","Normal secondary","Wedge-shaped discs; head control, walking"],["Hyperkyphosis","Sagittal, exaggerated","Abnormal","Osteoporotic wedge fractures; spinal tuberculosis"],["Hyperlordosis","Sagittal, exaggerated","Abnormal","Forward center of gravity: pregnancy, obesity"],["Scoliosis","Coronal curve plus axial rotation","Abnormal","Idiopathic; congenital; neuromuscular"]],"caption":"Kyphosis and lordosis are front to back curves; scoliosis is a side to side curve with rotation."},{"type":"flow","title":"From severe thoracic scoliosis to cor pulmonale","steps":["Thoracic curve with vertebral and rib rotation","Chest wall stiffens; inspiratory muscles disadvantaged","TLC and vital capacity fall; FEV1/FVC preserved","Alveolar hypoventilation: high PaCO2, low PaO2","Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction; pulmonary hypertension","Right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale)"],"caption":"Restriction arises in the chest wall, not the lung tissue, so airflow ratios stay normal."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 19-year-old with a 95 degree thoracic scoliosis has spirometry. Predict FVC, FEV1, and FEV1/FVC, and say how the pattern differs from COPD.","explain":"FVC and FEV1 both fall, FEV1/FVC is normal or high, and TLC is low (extrapulmonary restriction). COPD lowers FEV1 more than FVC, so the ratio falls and TLC is normal or high.","check":"The paragraph on scoliosis and the lungs, and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C03","obj":"MS-3.3","title":"Skull foramina, their contents, and the pterion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cranial base steps down in three fossae. Openings in the sphenoid lead to the orbit, face, and jaw; posterior fossa openings carry the lower cranial nerves, the great veins, and the brainstem."},{"type":"p","text":"All three nerves that move the eye and the ophthalmic nerve, which carries sensation from the eyeball and forehead, enter the orbit through the superior orbital fissure, between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid: the oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV), ophthalmic (V1), and abducens (VI) nerves, with the superior ophthalmic vein draining back to the cavernous sinus. A lesion here causes ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, loss of pupillary constriction, and forehead and corneal numbness, but spares vision because the optic nerve runs separately through the optic canal. The maxillary nerve (V2) runs forward through the foramen rotundum to the pterygopalatine fossa, and the mandibular nerve (V3), the only trigeminal division with motor fibers, drops through the foramen ovale to the infratemporal fossa. Just behind the ovale, the foramen spinosum admits the middle meningeal artery."},{"type":"p","text":"In the posterior fossa, the facial (VII) and vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerves enter the internal acoustic meatus. VII then runs through the facial canal and exits at the stylomastoid foramen, so a vestibular schwannoma in the meatus causes one-sided hearing loss and tinnitus before facial weakness. The jugular foramen transmits IX, X, and XI with the internal jugular vein, and the foramen magnum transmits the medulla, the vertebral arteries, and the spinal roots of XI, so tonsils herniating through it compress the medullary respiratory centers."},{"type":"table","title":"Major skull openings and their contents","cols":["Opening","Contents","Deficit when damaged"],"rows":[["Optic canal","CN II, ophthalmic artery","Monocular vision loss"],["Superior orbital fissure","CN III, IV, V1, VI; superior ophthalmic vein","Ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, numb forehead"],["Foramen rotundum","CN V2","Numb cheek and upper lip"],["Foramen ovale","CN V3 (motor and sensory)","Weak chewing, numb jaw"],["Foramen spinosum","Middle meningeal artery","Epidural hematoma if torn"],["Internal acoustic meatus","CN VII, CN VIII, labyrinthine artery","Hearing loss, vertigo, facial weakness"],["Jugular foramen","CN IX, X, XI; internal jugular vein","Dysphagia, hoarseness, lost gag, weak shrug"],["Hypoglossal canal","CN XII","Tongue deviates to lesion side"],["Foramen magnum","Medulla, vertebral arteries, spinal roots of XI","Medullary compression"]],"caption":"Nerves sharing an opening fail together, so a grouped deficit points to one site."},{"type":"p","text":"The pterion is the H-shaped junction where the frontal, parietal, and squamous temporal bones meet the greater wing of the sphenoid, a short distance above the zygomatic arch. The bone is thin, and on its inner surface, in a groove or sometimes a bony canal, runs the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery, a branch of the maxillary artery that entered through the foramen spinosum. A blow to the temple fractures this thin plate, and the fracture edge tears the artery. Arterial blood strips the periosteal dura from bone, but that layer is fused firmly at the sutures, so the collection is biconvex (lens-shaped) and does not cross suture lines, unlike the crescent of a venous subdural hematoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"From pterion fracture to herniation","steps":["Blow to the temple fractures the thin pterion","Fracture edge tears the middle meningeal artery","Lens-shaped hematoma, bounded by the sutures","Uncal herniation compresses the same-side CN III"],"caption":"Arterial bleeding enlarges the hematoma quickly."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning from a temple injury","text":"A 17-year-old struck on the left temple by a baseball is briefly unconscious, then lucid. Three hours later he is drowsy, with a dilated, unreactive left pupil and right-sided weakness. The temple overlies the pterion, so the likely injury is a fracture tearing the middle meningeal artery. The brief unconsciousness is concussion; the hematoma then needs time to strip dura from bone and reach a damaging size, hence the lucid interval. The growing mass pushes the left uncus over the tentorium, compressing the left oculomotor nerve (superficial parasympathetic fibers first) and the left cerebral peduncle, whose corticospinal fibers serve the right side."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C04","obj":"MS-3.4","title":"Bones of the upper limb and the nerves at risk in humeral fractures","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The upper limb's only bony link to the trunk is the clavicle at the sternoclavicular joint. The S-shaped clavicle is a strut that holds the limb away from the trunk and passes force to the axial skeleton; it often fractures, after which the sternocleidomastoid lifts the medial fragment and the limb's weight drops the shoulder. The scapula glides on the chest wall; its acromion joins the clavicle, and its shallow glenoid gives the shoulder great range but little bony stability. The humerus has a head, anatomical neck, greater and lesser tubercles for rotator cuff tendons, and the narrower surgical neck below them; the shaft bears the deltoid tuberosity and radial groove, and the distal end the capitulum (for the radius), trochlea (for the ulna), and two epicondyles."},{"type":"p","text":"In the forearm the radius lies laterally (thumb side) and the ulna medially. The ulna is large at the elbow, where the olecranon and trochlear notch grip the trochlea of the humerus as a hinge, and small at the wrist. The radius is small at the elbow, where its head spins in the annular ligament during pronation and supination, and large at the wrist, where it carries the scaphoid and lunate. The carpals form two rows (proximal: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform; distal: trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate) arched into the carpal tunnel. The scaphoid bridges both rows and is the carpal most often fractured; its vessels enter mainly distally, so a waist fracture can leave the proximal pole without blood, causing nonunion and avascular necrosis. The lunate, the carpal most often dislocated, can compress the median nerve in the tunnel."},{"type":"p","text":"Three nerves lie directly on the humerus, so the fracture site predicts the nerve. The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck, which often breaks when an older adult with osteoporosis falls; injury weakens the deltoid and teres minor (abduction beyond the first 15 degrees) and numbs skin over the lower deltoid. The radial nerve spirals along the radial groove on the back of the shaft, so a midshaft fracture paralyzes the wrist and finger extensors (wrist drop) and numbs the back of the hand near the first web space; triceps is usually spared because most of its branches leave the nerve above the groove. The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle, so a fracture there weakens most intrinsic hand muscles, giving weak finger abduction and adduction, clawing of the ring and little fingers, and numbness of the medial one and a half digits. The clawing is often milder than with a wrist lesion because an elbow lesion also weakens the medial flexor digitorum profundus, which would otherwise flex the distal joints."},{"type":"table","title":"Humeral and carpal fracture sites and the structures at risk","cols":["Fracture site","Structure at risk","Main deficit or result"],"rows":[["Surgical neck of humerus","Axillary nerve, posterior circumflex humeral artery","Weak abduction beyond 15 degrees; numb lower deltoid skin"],["Midshaft humerus (radial groove)","Radial nerve, deep brachial artery","Wrist drop; numb dorsal first web space; triceps usually spared"],["Supracondylar humerus","Median nerve, brachial artery","Weak thumb opposition and flexion; forearm ischemia"],["Medial epicondyle","Ulnar nerve","Weak intrinsic hand muscles; ring and little finger claw"],["Scaphoid waist","Retrograde blood supply to proximal pole","Nonunion, avascular necrosis"],["Lunate dislocation","Median nerve in carpal tunnel","Numb lateral digits; weak thenar muscles"]],"caption":"Each nerve is injured where it lies directly against bone."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the nerve from the fracture","text":"A 45-year-old falls from a ladder and has a midshaft fracture of the humerus. He cannot extend his wrist or fingers but can extend his elbow, and the back of the hand near the thumb is numb. The radial nerve lies in the radial groove on the back of the midshaft, so it is the nerve at risk. Wrist and finger extensors are supplied below the groove and are lost; triceps branches mostly leave the nerve higher, so elbow extension survives."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C05","obj":"MS-3.5","title":"Bones of the lower limb, the foot arches, and the common fibular nerve","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each hip bone forms from the ilium, ischium, and pubis, which meet in the acetabulum and are joined by a Y-shaped (triradiate) cartilage until they fuse in adolescence. With the sacrum and pubic symphysis, the hip bones form a ring that carries weight from the spine to the femurs in standing and to the ischial tuberosities in sitting. The deep acetabulum and its labrum hold most of the femoral head, trading range for stability."},{"type":"p","text":"The femur is the longest and strongest bone. Its neck meets the shaft at about 125 degrees in adults, giving hip muscles leverage, and the shaft angles medially so the knees sit under the body. Most blood reaches the adult femoral head through retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery running up the neck inside the capsule, so a displaced neck fracture risks avascular necrosis of the head. The tibia carries body weight from the femur to the talus and, because its anteromedial surface lies under the skin, often has open fractures; the slender fibula bears little weight, anchors muscles, and forms the lateral malleolus."},{"type":"p","text":"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve, a terminal branch of the sciatic nerve, winds around the fibular neck under only skin and fascia. A fibular neck fracture, a tight cast, or habitual leg crossing can injure it. Its deep branch supplies the ankle dorsiflexors and toe extensors and the skin of the first web space, and its superficial branch supplies the evertors (fibularis longus and brevis) and most skin of the dorsum of the foot. Injury therefore causes foot drop with a high-stepping gait, loss of eversion, and numbness over the dorsum of the foot, while plantar flexion and inversion by tibialis posterior, both supplied by the tibial nerve, remain."},{"type":"p","text":"The foot bones form arches that spread weight between heel and metatarsal heads and let the foot act as a shock absorber in stance and a rigid lever at push-off. The medial longitudinal arch is the highest, with the head of the talus as its keystone. It is held passively by the plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament, which cradles the talar head, and by the plantar aponeurosis, which ties heel to toes like a bowstring, and actively by the tibialis posterior tendon. When the toes dorsiflex at push-off, the aponeurosis winds around the metatarsal heads, shortening the foot and raising the arch."},{"type":"p","text":"Pes planus (flat foot) is a low or collapsed medial longitudinal arch. Infants look flat-footed because of a plantar fat pad, and a flexible flat foot whose arch reappears on tiptoe is usually painless. Acquired adult flat foot most often follows tibialis posterior tendon failure (below); a rigid flat foot, as with fused tarsals (tarsal coalition), has a structural cause."},{"type":"table","title":"Foot arches and their supports","cols":["Arch","Bones","Main supports"],"rows":[["Medial longitudinal","Calcaneus, talus, navicular, cuneiforms, metatarsals 1 to 3","Spring ligament, plantar aponeurosis, tibialis posterior"],["Lateral longitudinal","Calcaneus, cuboid, metatarsals 4 and 5","Long and short plantar ligaments, fibularis longus, plantar aponeurosis"],["Transverse","Cuboid, cuneiforms, metatarsal bases","Fibularis longus tendon crossing the sole, intrinsic muscles"]],"caption":"The high medial arch is the one that collapses in pes planus."},{"type":"flow","title":"From tibialis posterior failure to acquired flat foot","steps":["Tibialis posterior tendon degenerates and lengthens","Spring ligament takes the load and stretches","Talar head drops medially and toward the sole","Medial longitudinal arch collapses","Heel tilts into valgus; forefoot abducts","Medial pain; weak single-heel rise"],"caption":"Dynamic support fails first, then passive support."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After a tight below-knee cast, a patient catches his toes when walking. Which movements are weak, which are preserved, and where is skin numb?","explain":"Pressure at the fibular neck injures the common fibular nerve. Dorsiflexion and toe extension (deep branch) and eversion (superficial branch) are weak; plantar flexion and inversion by tibialis posterior (tibial nerve) remain. The dorsum of the foot is numb.","check":"The common fibular nerve paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and histology explain much of the anatomy. The dens is the displaced body of C1, primary and secondary curves record fetal flexion and later upright posture, and hemivertebrae from defective somite segmentation (MS-1) cause congenital scoliosis. Vertebral bodies, the femoral neck, and the distal radius are rich in trabecular bone, which osteoporosis thins first (MS-13-C), so they are where fragility fractures cluster. Blood supply that enters bone from one end, as in the scaphoid and femoral head, explains avascular necrosis after fracture, and infection or inflammation that erodes ligaments, such as rheumatoid destruction of the transverse ligament of the atlas (MS-11), turns a stable joint into a threat to the spinal cord."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology follow from position. A nerve or vessel that lies on bone or passes through a foramen is injured when that bone breaks or the opening narrows, so a grouped deficit points to one site: III, IV, V1, and VI to the superior orbital fissure; IX, X, and XI to the jugular foramen; extensors with spared triceps to the radial groove. Severe scoliosis links the skeleton to respiratory and cardiac physiology through restriction, hypoventilation, and hypoxic pulmonary hypertension. Anatomical variation limits these rules: the vertebral artery sometimes enters above C6, and the middle meningeal artery may run in a bony canal, so the expected structure is the most likely one, not a certainty."}],"summary":["Cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina for the vertebral artery (usually C6 to C1) and bifid spines; the atlas has no body, and the dens of the axis is the pivot for head rotation.","Thoracic costal facets carry the ribs, and the rib cage limits thoracic motion; large lumbar bodies bear weight and sagittal lumbar facets permit flexion and extension but block rotation.","Thoracic and sacral kyphoses are primary curves; cervical and lumbar lordoses are secondary curves formed with head control and walking.","Scoliosis is lateral curvature with vertebral rotation; hyperkyphosis is an exaggerated sagittal curve, often from osteoporotic wedge fractures.","Severe thoracic scoliosis stiffens the chest wall, lowering TLC and vital capacity with normal FEV1/FVC, and can progress to hypoventilation and cor pulmonale.","Superior orbital fissure: III, IV, V1, VI. Rotundum: V2. Ovale: V3. Spinosum: middle meningeal artery. Internal acoustic meatus: VII and VIII. Jugular foramen: IX, X, XI and internal jugular vein. Foramen magnum: medulla and vertebral arteries.","A fracture of the thin pterion tears the middle meningeal artery, producing a biconvex epidural hematoma that does not cross sutures and can cause uncal herniation.","Humeral surgical neck fractures injure the axillary nerve, midshaft fractures the radial nerve (wrist drop), and medial epicondyle fractures the ulnar nerve.","Scaphoid waist and femoral neck fractures risk avascular necrosis because blood reaches the vulnerable fragment from one direction only.","Fibular neck fractures injure the common fibular nerve (foot drop, lost eversion); collapse of the medial longitudinal arch, often from tibialis posterior failure, causes pes planus."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back: vertebral column, regional vertebrae, and curvatures"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head: cranium, cranial base foramina, and meninges"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb: bones of the upper limb and their clinical box notes"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb: bones of the lower limb, common fibular nerve, and arches of the foot"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors; Central Nervous System, section on epidural hematoma"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease: restrictive lung disease"}]},"MS-4":{"unit":"MS-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01","title":"Joints: Structure, Innervation, and Function","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how joints are built, how they are innervated, and how three clinically important joints, the shoulder, knee, and temporomandibular joint, stay stable while they move. Joint classification follows from the tissue between the bones and the shape of the articular surfaces. Hilton law explains joint position sense, protective reflexes, and referred joint pain. Board questions usually describe a mechanism of injury, a positive examination test, a lost movement, or pain felt away from its source, and ask you to name the structure or nerve responsible.","prerequisites":["Connective tissue types: dense regular tissue, hyaline cartilage, and fibrocartilage","Brachial and lumbar plexus organization and the cords and branches that leave them","Muscle actions at the shoulder, hip, knee, and jaw, with their nerve supply","Sensory receptors and the dorsal horn pathways for proprioception and pain","Branches of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3)"],"connections":[{"to":"Bone and cartilage histology (MS-2)","how":"Avascular articular cartilage depends on synovial fluid for nutrition, which explains why it heals poorly once injured."},{"to":"Muscles of the limbs (MS-5)","how":"Muscles that cross a joint both move it and stabilize it, and their nerves also supply its capsule."},{"to":"Neuroscience: somatosensory pathways","how":"Convergence of joint and skin afferents on shared spinal neurons is the basis of referred joint pain."},{"to":"Rheumatology and joint pathology","how":"Synovium, cartilage, and capsule are the targets of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, crystal disease, and septic arthritis."},{"to":"Head and neck anatomy","how":"The TMJ shares its nerve supply with the masseter and the skin of the ear and temple, so jaw disease can present as ear pain."}],"terms":[{"term":"Synarthrosis","def":"A joint that permits essentially no movement, such as a cranial suture."},{"term":"Amphiarthrosis","def":"A joint that permits slight movement, such as the pubic symphysis or an intervertebral disc."},{"term":"Diarthrosis","def":"A freely movable joint; all diarthroses are synovial joints."},{"term":"Synovial membrane","def":"The vascular connective tissue lining of a joint capsule whose cells secrete hyaluronan and lubricin into synovial fluid."},{"term":"Degrees of freedom","def":"The number of independent axes about which a joint can rotate: one for a hinge or pivot, two for condyloid and saddle joints, three for a ball and socket."},{"term":"Hilton law","def":"The nerves supplying the muscles that move a joint also supply the joint capsule and the skin over the muscles' insertions."},{"term":"Referred pain","def":"Pain perceived at a site other than its source because both sites send afferents to the same spinal cord neurons."},{"term":"Rotator cuff","def":"Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, whose tendons blend with the capsule and press the humeral head into the glenoid."},{"term":"Glenoid labrum","def":"A fibrocartilage rim attached to the margin of the glenoid fossa that deepens the socket slightly."},{"term":"Menisci","def":"Crescent-shaped fibrocartilage pads between the femoral condyles and tibial plateau that improve fit and spread load."},{"term":"Valgus and varus","def":"Valgus angulation points the distal segment away from the midline (knee drawn inward); varus points it toward the midline (knee bowed outward)."},{"term":"Articular disc","def":"A plate of dense fibrous tissue or fibrocartilage inside a synovial joint, such as the TMJ, that divides the cavity into two compartments."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C01","obj":"MS-4.1","title":"Classifying joints by function and synovial joints by shape","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A joint is any place where two or more bones (or a bone and cartilage) meet. How much it moves depends on what fills the space between the bones. When dense fibrous tissue or cartilage binds the bones directly, the surfaces cannot slide on one another, and motion is absent or slight. When the bones are separated by a fluid-filled cavity enclosed in a capsule, the surfaces are free to move. Two classifications follow from this: a structural one (fibrous, cartilaginous, synovial) and a functional one based on mobility."},{"type":"table","title":"Functional classes and the structures that produce them","cols":["Functional class","Mobility","Structural basis","Examples"],"rows":[["Synarthrosis","Immovable","Fibrous (suture, gomphosis) or primary cartilaginous (synchondrosis of hyaline cartilage)","Cranial sutures, tooth in its socket, epiphyseal growth plate"],["Amphiarthrosis","Slightly movable","Secondary cartilaginous (symphysis of fibrocartilage) or a fibrous syndesmosis","Pubic symphysis, intervertebral disc, distal tibiofibular joint"],["Diarthrosis","Freely movable","Synovial: capsule, synovial membrane, cavity, articular cartilage","Shoulder, hip, knee, elbow, TMJ"]],"caption":"Every diarthrosis is synovial. Synchondroses are usually temporary and ossify after growth ends."},{"type":"p","text":"A synovial joint has a fibrous capsule continuous with the periosteum, an inner synovial membrane that lines everything in the cavity except the articular cartilage, and a thin film of synovial fluid. Synoviocytes secrete hyaluronan, which makes the fluid viscous, and lubricin, which reduces friction at the cartilage surface. Articular cartilage is usually hyaline, has no blood vessels or nerves, and receives its nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid, aided by the cyclic compression of movement. Some synovial joints (TMJ, sternoclavicular) contain an articular disc, and the knee contains menisci."},{"type":"p","text":"Among synovial joints, the shape of the articular surfaces sets the axes of motion. A cylinder in a trough (hinge) or a peg in a ring (pivot) allows rotation about one axis. An oval convex surface in an oval concavity (condyloid, also called ellipsoid) allows flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction but not axial rotation. A saddle joint has two surfaces each convex in one direction and concave in the other, giving two axes. A sphere in a cup (ball and socket) allows three axes, including rotation. Flat surfaces (plane joints) only glide, with motion limited by ligaments."},{"type":"table","title":"Synovial joint types","cols":["Type","Axes","Movement","Examples"],"rows":[["Hinge","1","Flexion and extension","Humeroulnar (elbow), interphalangeal, talocrural (ankle); knee is a modified hinge"],["Pivot","1","Rotation about a long axis","Median atlantoaxial (dens in ring of atlas and transverse ligament), proximal radioulnar (radial head in annular ligament)"],["Condyloid (ellipsoid)","2","Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, circumduction","Radiocarpal (wrist), metacarpophalangeal, atlanto-occipital"],["Saddle","2","Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction; opposition with conjunct rotation","First carpometacarpal (trapezium and first metacarpal)"],["Ball and socket","3","All of the above plus medial and lateral rotation","Glenohumeral, hip"],["Plane","Gliding","Sliding of flat surfaces","Acromioclavicular, intercarpal, zygapophysial (facet) joints"]],"caption":"Number of axes rises with the curvature of the articular surfaces."},{"type":"p","text":"Two examples are tested often. At the median atlantoaxial joint the dens of C2 sits in a ring formed by the anterior arch of the atlas and the transverse ligament; the atlas and skull turn around the dens, giving roughly half of cervical rotation. Rupture of the transverse ligament, as in rheumatoid arthritis, lets the dens move back toward the spinal cord. At the first carpometacarpal joint the saddle shape lets the thumb flex across the palm while it rotates medially, so the thumb pad can meet each fingertip in opposition. In general, mobility costs stability: the shallow ball and socket of the shoulder moves most and dislocates most."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C02","obj":"MS-4.2","title":"Hilton law, articular sensation, and referred joint pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hilton law states that the nerves supplying the muscles that move a joint also supply the joint itself and the skin over those muscles' insertions. Anatomically, articular branches usually arise from the nerves to nearby muscles as those nerves cross the joint. The muscle, the joint it moves, and the overlying skin therefore share nerves and spinal segments, which lets the joint report its state directly to the reflex circuits that control the muscles around it."},{"type":"p","text":"Articular nerves are richest in the fibrous capsule, ligaments, and synovial membrane. Articular cartilage has no nerves, so cartilage loss in osteoarthritis is painless in itself; the pain comes from subchondral bone, synovium, and the stretched capsule. Joint receptors work with muscle spindles to give position and movement sense. Joint receptors signal mainly near the ends of the range, and spindles supply most mid-range position sense."},{"type":"table","title":"Articular receptors","cols":["Receptor","Location","Adaptation","Signal"],"rows":[["Ruffini endings","Fibrous capsule","Slow","Joint position and capsular tension"],["Pacinian corpuscles","Capsule and fat pads","Rapid","Onset of movement and acceleration"],["Golgi-type endings","Ligaments","Slow","Tension near the extremes of motion"],["Free nerve endings (A-delta and C fibers)","Capsule, ligaments, synovium, fat pads","Variable; sensitized by inflammation","Nociception from stretch, injury, and inflammatory mediators"]],"caption":"Inflammatory mediators such as bradykinin and prostaglandins lower the threshold of joint nociceptors, so normal movement becomes painful."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the same afferents feed motor reflexes, capsular stretch or inflammation reflexly inhibits the muscles around the joint. Swelling of the knee, for example, inhibits the quadriceps (arthrogenic muscle inhibition), and a painful joint is held in the position of greatest capsular volume. When articular sensation is lost, as in diabetic neuropathy, tabes dorsalis, or syringomyelia, the joint is repeatedly overloaded without warning and can be destroyed (neuropathic or Charcot joint)."},{"type":"p","text":"Shared innervation also explains referred pain. The hip capsule is supplied by the femoral nerve anteriorly, the obturator nerve inferomedially, the nerve to quadratus femoris posteriorly, and the superior gluteal nerve superiorly. The femoral and obturator nerves (L2 to L4) also supply the knee and the skin of the anterior and medial thigh. Afferents from the hip and from these skin areas converge on the same dorsal horn neurons, and the brain, more used to signals from skin, projects the pain to the thigh and knee. A child or adolescent with knee pain, such as from slipped capital femoral epiphysis or Legg-Calve-Perthes disease, needs a hip examination."},{"type":"flow","title":"How hip disease is felt in the knee","steps":["Hip capsule is inflamed or stretched","Femoral and obturator nociceptors fire","Afferents enter L2 to L4 dorsal horn","Converge on neurons that also serve thigh and knee","Brain maps the signal to the familiar skin area","Pain is felt in the medial thigh and knee"],"caption":"Convergence on shared spinal neurons, not nerve damage, produces the referral."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An obese 13-year-old limps and complains only of left knee pain. The knee examination is normal. Which joint should be examined next, and through which nerves is the pain referred?","explain":"The left hip. Slipped capital femoral epiphysis stretches the hip capsule, and its nociceptive input travels in the obturator and femoral nerves, which also supply the knee and medial thigh, so pain is projected there.","check":"The hip innervation paragraph and the referral flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C03","obj":"MS-4.3","title":"Glenohumeral stability, anterior dislocation, and the axillary nerve","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The glenohumeral joint is the most mobile joint in the body and the one most often dislocated. Its bony fit is poor: the glenoid fossa is shallow and covers only about a quarter to a third of the humeral head at any moment, and the fibrocartilage labrum deepens it only slightly. The capsule is loose enough to allow wide range, so it is taut only near the extremes of motion. Stability through most of the range therefore comes from muscle rather than from bone or ligament."},{"type":"table","title":"Stabilizers of the glenohumeral joint","cols":["Structure","Type","Nerve","How it stabilizes"],"rows":[["Glenoid labrum","Static","None (passive)","Deepens the socket and adds a suction seal"],["Capsule and glenohumeral ligaments","Static","None (passive)","Inferior glenohumeral ligament resists anterior translation in abduction and external rotation"],["Supraspinatus","Dynamic","Suprascapular (C5, C6)","Compresses the head into the glenoid; initiates abduction (about 0 to 15 degrees)"],["Infraspinatus","Dynamic","Suprascapular (C5, C6)","Lateral rotation; resists posterior and superior translation"],["Teres minor","Dynamic","Axillary (C5, C6)","Lateral rotation; depresses the head"],["Subscapularis","Dynamic","Upper and lower subscapular (C5, C6)","Medial rotation; anterior wall of the joint"]],"caption":"The four cuff tendons blend with the capsule superiorly, anteriorly, and posteriorly, but not inferiorly."},{"type":"p","text":"The rotator cuff works by concavity compression. Pulling the humeral head into the glenoid keeps its center fixed while it rotates. This is essential during abduction, because the deltoid's line of pull at low angles would otherwise slide the head upward against the acromion. The cuff and deltoid act as a force couple: the deltoid elevates the arm while the cuff holds the fulcrum in place. A torn cuff therefore causes weakness, superior migration of the head, and impingement even when the deltoid is intact."},{"type":"p","text":"The inferior capsule has no cuff tendon over it and is the weakest part of the joint. With the arm abducted and externally rotated, as in blocking a throw or falling on an outstretched hand, the head is levered forward and down through the anteroinferior capsule and comes to rest below the coracoid process. The shoulder looks squared off because the head no longer fills the deltoid beneath the acromion. Anterior dislocation may tear the anteroinferior labrum (Bankart lesion) or impact the posterolateral humeral head on the glenoid rim (Hill-Sachs lesion), both of which favor recurrence. Posterior dislocation is uncommon and classically follows a seizure or electric shock, when the strong medial rotators overpower the lateral rotators."},{"type":"p","text":"The axillary nerve (C5, C6, posterior cord) passes through the quadrangular space with the posterior circumflex humeral artery and winds around the surgical neck of the humerus, directly below the joint. The displaced head stretches it, as can a fracture of the surgical neck. The result is predictable from what the nerve supplies: the deltoid, teres minor, and skin over the lower deltoid through the superior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm. Numbness over this patch is the quickest bedside sign of axillary nerve injury."},{"type":"flow","title":"Anterior dislocation to axillary nerve palsy","steps":["Arm abducted and externally rotated under force","Head levered through weak anteroinferior capsule","Head lies below the coracoid process","Axillary nerve stretched around the surgical neck","Deltoid and teres minor denervated","Weak abduction past 15 degrees; numb lower deltoid skin"],"caption":"Supraspinatus can still start abduction, and infraspinatus masks most of the lateral rotation loss."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After reduction of an anterior dislocation, a patient can begin abducting the arm but cannot raise it further, and lateral rotation is only slightly weak. Which nerve is injured, and why is the first part of abduction spared?","explain":"The axillary nerve. Supraspinatus, supplied by the suprascapular nerve, initiates abduction and is intact, but the deltoid, the main abductor from about 15 to 90 degrees, is paralyzed. Infraspinatus, also suprascapular, still performs most lateral rotation, so loss of teres minor is subtle.","check":"The stabilizer table and the axillary nerve paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C04","obj":"MS-4.4","title":"Knee ligaments, menisci, and the tests that examine them","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The knee is a modified hinge: rounded femoral condyles sit on a nearly flat tibial plateau, so bone shape adds little stability, and flexion and extension come with a small amount of rotation. Four main ligaments and two menisci keep it aligned. Each ligament resists one direction of displacement, so each test applies that displacement and looks for excess movement or a soft end point."},{"type":"p","text":"The cruciate ligaments lie inside the capsule but outside the synovial cavity and are named for their tibial attachments. The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) runs from the anterior intercondylar area of the tibia upward, backward, and laterally to the medial surface of the lateral femoral condyle; it stops the tibia sliding forward on the femur and limits hyperextension. The posterior cruciate ligament (PCL), stronger and thicker, runs from the posterior intercondylar area upward and forward to the lateral surface of the medial femoral condyle; it stops the tibia sliding backward and is the main stabilizer of the flexed, weight-bearing knee. The medial (tibial) collateral ligament is a broad flat band whose deep fibers attach to the capsule and medial meniscus. The lateral (fibular) collateral ligament is a round cord to the fibular head, separated from the lateral meniscus by the popliteus tendon."},{"type":"table","title":"Knee structures and their tests","cols":["Structure","Resists","Test","Positive finding"],"rows":[["ACL","Anterior tibial translation","Lachman (knee at 20 to 30 degrees); anterior drawer (90 degrees)","Excess forward glide of the tibia with a soft end point"],["PCL","Posterior tibial translation","Posterior drawer (90 degrees); posterior sag","Tibia pushes or sags backward"],["MCL","Valgus force (opening of the medial side)","Valgus stress at 20 to 30 degrees","Medial joint line gapes"],["LCL","Varus force (opening of the lateral side)","Varus stress at 20 to 30 degrees","Lateral joint line gapes"],["Menisci","Load spreading; torn by compression with rotation","McMurray; joint line tenderness","Painful click or catch on rotation"]],"caption":"At 20 to 30 degrees of flexion a collateral can be judged alone; gaping in full extension implies cruciate or capsular injury too."},{"type":"p","text":"Lachman is more sensitive than the anterior drawer, particularly soon after injury. At 90 degrees, hamstring guarding pulls the tibia back and the posterior horn of the medial meniscus wedges against the femoral condyle, blocking forward glide; at 20 to 30 degrees neither happens."},{"type":"p","text":"The menisci are fibrocartilage wedges that deepen the plateau and spread load; only their outer rim is vascular, so inner tears heal poorly. The C-shaped medial meniscus is fixed to the deep MCL and capsule, so it cannot shift away when the femur rotates and compresses it, and the medial compartment also carries more load. The nearly circular lateral meniscus is free of the LCL and is pulled back by popliteus during flexion. The medial meniscus therefore tears more often. Swelling timing helps localize injury: the ACL carries a branch of the middle genicular artery, so its rupture causes hemarthrosis within hours, whereas an isolated meniscal tear swells more slowly."},{"type":"flow","title":"The unhappy triad from a lateral blow","steps":["Blow to lateral knee, foot planted, knee slightly flexed","Valgus force opens the medial side","MCL tears","Continued valgus with rotation ruptures the ACL","Medial meniscus, fixed to the MCL, may also tear"],"caption":"In noncontact pivoting ACL injuries, the lateral meniscus is often the one torn."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a knee examination","text":"A skier twists a planted knee, hears a pop, and the knee swells within 2 hours. At 25 degrees of flexion the tibia glides forward with no firm end point, while the anterior drawer at 90 degrees is equivocal. Valgus stress at 25 degrees opens the medial joint line. Rapid swelling means a vascular structure bled, most often the ACL, and the positive Lachman confirms it; guarding and the meniscal block masked the drawer. Medial gapping means a torn MCL, and medial joint line tenderness with a painful McMurray would add the medial meniscus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C05","obj":"MS-4.5","title":"The temporomandibular joint and disc displacement","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is a synovial joint between the head (condyle) of the mandible and the mandibular fossa and articular eminence of the temporal bone. Unlike most synovial joints, its articular surfaces are covered by fibrocartilage rather than hyaline cartilage. An articular disc of dense fibrous tissue divides the cavity into two separate synovial compartments. The disc is biconcave, with thick anterior and posterior bands and a thin intermediate zone on which the condyle normally sits. Behind it, a vascular, richly innervated retrodiscal tissue (bilaminar zone) attaches it to the temporal bone and condyle, and in front the superior head of the lateral pterygoid inserts into the disc and capsule. The lateral (temporomandibular) ligament thickens the capsule and limits posterior displacement of the condyle."},{"type":"table","title":"The two TMJ compartments","cols":["Compartment","Between","Movement","Role"],"rows":[["Lower (inferior)","Condyle and disc","Hinge rotation","Early mouth opening"],["Upper (superior)","Disc and temporal bone","Gliding (translation) forward onto the eminence","Wide opening, protrusion, and side-to-side grinding"]],"caption":"Wide opening requires both compartments working in sequence."},{"type":"flow","title":"Opening the mouth at the TMJ","steps":["Digastric and other suprahyoid muscles depress the mandible","Condyle rotates under the disc (lower compartment)","Lateral pterygoid pulls condyle and disc forward","Disc and condyle glide onto the eminence (upper compartment)","Full opening; condyle sits under the eminence"],"caption":"Closing reverses the sequence, driven by temporalis, masseter, and medial pterygoid, with posterior temporalis fibers retracting the condyle."},{"type":"p","text":"The joint is supplied by the auriculotemporal nerve, the main supply to the posterior and lateral capsule, and by the masseteric nerve anteriorly, both branches of CN V3. This fits Hilton law: the masseteric nerve supplies the masseter that moves the joint. The auriculotemporal nerve also supplies the skin of the temple, tragus, and external acoustic meatus, so TMJ pain is commonly felt in front of or inside the ear. Because the lateral pterygoids pull the condyles forward, a unilateral CN V3 lesion makes the jaw deviate toward the paralyzed side on opening."},{"type":"p","text":"Disc displacement is usually anterior or anteromedial, in the direction of the lateral pterygoid's pull, once the retrodiscal tissue has been stretched. With the disc ahead of the condyle, the condyle rests on the retrodiscal tissue, whose compression and inflammation cause pain. In displacement with reduction, the condyle jumps over the thick posterior band onto the intermediate zone during opening, which produces a click, and the disc slips off again near the end of closing, sometimes with a softer second click (reciprocal clicking). In displacement without reduction, the disc stays in front and blocks forward translation, so the click disappears, opening is limited (closed lock), and the jaw deviates toward the affected side because that condyle cannot glide. Dislocation is different: the condyle itself passes in front of the eminence, often during a yawn, and the mouth is locked open."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting a click and a lock","text":"A patient has had a painless click on opening the right jaw for months. One morning the click is gone, the mouth opens only partway, and the jaw swings to the right as it opens. Before, the disc was displaced anteriorly but recaptured each time the condyle crossed its posterior band, so the click marked reduction. Now the disc no longer reduces, so it blocks forward gliding of the right condyle. The left condyle still translates, which turns the mandible toward the right. Pain in front of the ear reflects compressed retrodiscal tissue supplied by the auriculotemporal nerve."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology explain how joints fail. Avascular, aneural articular cartilage and the avascular inner menisci heal poorly, so mechanical injury tends to progress to osteoarthritis, while the vascular, innervated synovium, capsule, and retrodiscal tissue are where pain, swelling, and inflammatory arthritis arise. Rheumatoid synovitis loosens ligaments such as the transverse ligament of the atlas, and loss of articular sensation in neuropathy removes the protective reflexes that Hilton law describes, leading to neuropathic joint destruction."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuroscience explains the clinical pattern. Joint, muscle, and skin afferents share segments, so the examiner must look beyond the painful region: knee pain may come from the hip, and ear pain from the TMJ. Examination tests are directional reasoning, not memorized lists, but they have limits: guarding, effusion, and combined injuries reduce their accuracy, and imaging confirms what the history and mechanism suggest."}],"summary":["Mobility depends on the tissue between bones: fibrous and cartilaginous joints are synarthroses or amphiarthroses, and all diarthroses are synovial.","Surface shape sets the axes: hinge and pivot one, condyloid and saddle two, ball and socket three, plane joints glide; the median atlantoaxial joint is a pivot and the first carpometacarpal joint a saddle.","Hilton law: nerves to the muscles that move a joint also supply the joint and overlying skin, linking articular sensation to protective muscle reflexes.","Hip pain referred to the knee travels in the femoral and obturator nerves (L2 to L4), which converge with knee and thigh afferents in the dorsal horn.","The shallow glenoid makes the shoulder depend on rotator cuff compression; the uncovered inferior capsule allows anteroinferior dislocation in abduction and external rotation.","Anterior dislocation stretches the axillary nerve: deltoid weakness beyond about 15 degrees of abduction, mild lateral rotation loss, and numbness over the lower deltoid.","ACL and PCL stop anterior and posterior tibial translation (Lachman and anterior drawer; posterior drawer); MCL and LCL resist valgus and varus stress.","The medial meniscus tears more often because its attachment to the MCL and capsule keeps it from moving out of the way.","The TMJ has fibrocartilage surfaces and a disc creating a lower hinge and upper gliding compartment; it is supplied by the auriculotemporal and masseteric branches of CN V3.","Anterior disc displacement with reduction clicks on opening; without reduction it causes closed lock, limited opening, and deviation toward the affected side."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Overview and Basic Concepts, section on joints"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb, glenohumeral joint and axillary nerve"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb, hip joint and knee joint"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, temporomandibular joint"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone, section on joints and synovial membrane"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Sensory transduction, somatic sensory receptors and proprioception"}]},"MS-5":{"unit":"MS-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01","title":"Muscles: Origin, Insertion, Action, and Innervation","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter teaches the muscles that board questions return to most often: the rotator cuff, the muscle groups served by the major limb nerves, the cranial nerve muscles of the head and neck, and the trunk muscles of posture, breathing, and the pelvic floor. The emphasis is on reasoning rather than memorizing lists. If you know where a muscle pulls, which compartment it lives in, and which nerve serves that compartment, you can work backward from a weakness, a deformity, or an abnormal gait to the injured nerve and the level of injury. Most exam items give exactly that kind of clue.","prerequisites":["Bones and bony landmarks of the scapula, humerus, pelvis, femur, tibia, and fibula","Brachial and lumbosacral plexus organization: roots, trunks or divisions, cords, and terminal branches","Joint movements and planes: flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation, inversion, eversion","Upper versus lower motor neuron signs","Pharyngeal arch and somite derivatives (embryology)"],"connections":[{"to":"Neuroscience: peripheral nerves and spinal cord","how":"Root, plexus, and peripheral nerve lesions each leave a distinct pattern of weakness, sensory loss, and reflex change."},{"to":"Embryology of the pharyngeal arches and somites","how":"A muscle keeps the nerve of the arch or somite it came from, which explains V3 to the chewing muscles, VII to the face, and XII to the tongue."},{"to":"Respiratory physiology","how":"The diaphragm generates most of the quiet tidal volume, so phrenic nerve and high cervical cord injuries present as ventilatory failure."},{"to":"Joints and connective tissue (musculoskeletal units)","how":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy and tears are degenerative tendon disorders shaped by the anatomy of the subacromial space."},{"to":"Reproductive and gastrointestinal systems","how":"The levator ani supports the pelvic organs and maintains the anorectal angle, linking pelvic floor injury to prolapse and incontinence."}],"terms":[{"term":"Origin","def":"The usually proximal, more fixed attachment of a muscle."},{"term":"Insertion","def":"The usually distal, more mobile attachment that the muscle pulls toward its origin."},{"term":"Compartment","def":"A group of muscles enclosed by fascia that share a function and, as a rule, a single motor nerve."},{"term":"Force couple","def":"Two muscles pulling in different directions on a bone so that their combined effect is rotation rather than translation."},{"term":"Rotator cuff","def":"The supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, whose tendons blend with the shoulder joint capsule and hold the humeral head in the glenoid."},{"term":"Subacromial space","def":"The gap between the humeral head and the coracoacromial arch, through which the supraspinatus tendon and subacromial bursa pass."},{"term":"Claw hand","def":"Hyperextension of the metacarpophalangeal joints with flexion of the interphalangeal joints, caused by loss of the lumbricals and interossei."},{"term":"Trendelenburg sign","def":"Drop of the pelvis on the unsupported side during single-leg stance, caused by weakness of the hip abductors on the stance side."},{"term":"Foot drop","def":"Inability to dorsiflex the ankle, so the toes catch the ground during the swing phase of gait."},{"term":"Dorsal ramus","def":"The posterior branch of a spinal nerve, supplying the deep (intrinsic) back muscles and the skin over them."},{"term":"Pelvic diaphragm","def":"The levator ani and coccygeus, which together form the muscular floor of the pelvic cavity."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C01","obj":"MS-5.1","title":"The rotator cuff","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The glenohumeral joint trades stability for range: a large humeral head sits on a shallow glenoid fossa, deepened only slightly by the glenoid labrum. The loose capsule cannot hold the head in place through the full arc of motion, so stability depends on four muscles whose tendons fuse with the capsule on its superior, posterior, and anterior sides. Together they form the rotator cuff (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis). Each muscle produces its own movement, but their shared job is to press the humeral head into the glenoid while larger muscles move the arm. The capsule is not reinforced by the cuff inferiorly, which is why most dislocations are anterior and inferior."},{"type":"table","title":"Rotator cuff muscles","cols":["Muscle","Origin","Insertion","Main action","Nerve (roots)"],"rows":[["Supraspinatus","Supraspinous fossa of scapula","Superior facet of greater tubercle","Initiates abduction (about the first 15 degrees)","Suprascapular (C5, C6)"],["Infraspinatus","Infraspinous fossa","Middle facet of greater tubercle","Lateral rotation","Suprascapular (C5, C6)"],["Teres minor","Middle part of lateral border of scapula","Inferior facet of greater tubercle","Lateral rotation","Axillary (C5, C6)"],["Subscapularis","Subscapular fossa (anterior surface)","Lesser tubercle","Medial rotation","Upper and lower subscapular (C5, C6)"]],"caption":"Three muscles reach the greater tubercle from behind; only subscapularis inserts on the lesser tubercle from the front."},{"type":"p","text":"Abduction shows why the cuff matters. With the arm at the side, the deltoid (axillary nerve) pulls almost straight upward along the humerus, so on its own it would slide the head up into the acromion rather than swing the arm out. The supraspinatus pulls the head medially into the glenoid, and the infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis pull it downward. This force couple converts the deltoid's pull into rotation. Classic teaching gives the supraspinatus the first 15 degrees and the deltoid the range to about 90 degrees; throughout the movement the trapezius and serratus anterior rotate the scapula upward, and that rotation is what allows the arm to rise above about 90 degrees."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the supraspinatus is the cuff tendon that fails","steps":["Tendon runs under the coracoacromial arch","Abduction and overhead use narrow the subacromial space","Tendon is compressed against the acromion repeatedly","Age-related degeneration weakens the tendon near its insertion","Tendinopathy, then partial or full-thickness tear","Painful arc; weak initiation of abduction"],"caption":"Impingement plus degeneration explains why supraspinatus tears are most common and rise with age."},{"type":"p","text":"Injury patterns follow the nerves. The suprascapular nerve passes through the suprascapular notch beneath the superior transverse scapular ligament, then around the spinoglenoid notch; entrapment at the first site weakens both abduction initiation and lateral rotation, while entrapment at the second weakens lateral rotation alone and wastes only the infraspinatus. The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck of the humerus, so a surgical neck fracture or anterior dislocation weakens the deltoid and teres minor and numbs the skin over the lower deltoid, sparing the supraspinatus. Infraspinatus remains a strong lateral rotator in that case, so lateral rotation is only mildly weak. Subscapularis loss is tested by the patient's inability to push the hand away from the lower back (lift-off test)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient cannot start abducting the arm from the side, but if the examiner lifts the arm to 30 degrees, the patient can continue to raise it. Which muscle is likely torn?","explain":"The supraspinatus. Once the arm is partly abducted, the deltoid has a better line of pull and can continue the movement even without the supraspinatus.","check":"The abduction paragraph and the force couple."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C02","obj":"MS-5.2","title":"Upper limb nerve injuries","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each upper limb compartment has a principal nerve: anterior arm (musculocutaneous), posterior arm and forearm (radial), most of the anterior forearm and thenar eminence (median), and most intrinsic hand muscles (ulnar). The lesion site along the nerve decides which branches are lost."},{"type":"table","title":"Upper limb nerves, muscles, and deficits","cols":["Nerve (roots)","Key muscles","Classic lesion and sign","Sensory loss"],"rows":[["Musculocutaneous (C5 to C7)","Biceps, brachialis, coracobrachialis","Weak elbow flexion and supination; lost biceps reflex","Lateral forearm"],["Radial (C5 to T1)","Triceps, brachioradialis, extensors, supinator","Midshaft humerus fracture: wrist drop","Dorsum of hand over first web space"],["Median (C6 to T1)","Most forearm flexors, thenar muscles, lateral two lumbricals","Wrist: ape hand. Elbow: also hand of benediction","Palmar lateral three and a half digits"],["Ulnar (C8, T1)","Flexor carpi ulnaris, medial profundus, hypothenar, interossei, medial lumbricals, adductor pollicis","Elbow or wrist: claw of digits 4 and 5, weak finger abduction","Medial one and a half digits"],["Long thoracic (C5 to C7)","Serratus anterior","Axillary surgery: winged scapula","None (purely motor)"]],"caption":"Root values vary slightly between texts."},{"type":"p","text":"Elbow flexion is weakened rather than abolished because the brachioradialis (radial nerve) still flexes the elbow, and supination survives through the supinator (radial nerve). The radial nerve spirals in the radial groove of the humerus, so a midshaft fracture paralyzes the wrist and finger extensors and the wrist hangs flexed (wrist drop); the triceps is usually spared because its branches leave above the groove. Compression in the axilla, as from a crutch, also weakens the triceps."},{"type":"p","text":"The median nerve enters the hand through the carpal tunnel and sends its recurrent branch to the thenar muscles. Compression there (carpal tunnel syndrome) or a wrist laceration weakens or paralyzes opposition and thumb abduction, the thenar eminence wastes, and the thumb lies in line with the fingers (ape hand). In carpal tunnel syndrome palm sensation is spared because the palmar cutaneous branch passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum. A lesion at the elbow, as in a supracondylar fracture, also paralyzes the flexor digitorum superficialis and the lateral half of the profundus, so on attempting a fist the index and middle fingers stay extended (hand of benediction)."},{"type":"flow","title":"How ulnar nerve loss produces a claw hand","steps":["Medial lumbricals and interossei are paralyzed","MCP flexion and IP extension of digits 4 and 5 are lost","Extensor digitorum hyperextends the MCP joints unopposed","Long flexors flex the IP joints","Claw of the ring and little fingers"],"caption":"Digits 2 and 3 are spared because the median nerve supplies their lumbricals."},{"type":"p","text":"The ulnar nerve also supplies all the interossei, so a card cannot be gripped between the fingers, and loss of adductor pollicis makes the patient flex the thumb tip with flexor pollicis longus (median nerve) to pinch paper (Froment sign). Clawing is paradoxically worse with a lesion at the wrist than at the elbow, because a distal lesion spares the medial flexor digitorum profundus, which then flexes the distal joints strongly. The long thoracic nerve lies on the surface of the serratus anterior in the medial axillary wall, exposed during axillary node dissection. The serratus holds the scapula against the chest wall and rotates it upward, so its loss lifts the medial border away when the patient pushes against a wall and limits abduction above the horizontal."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing an ulnar nerve lesion","text":"A cyclist has clawing of digits 4 and 5, weak finger abduction, and a positive Froment sign, with numbness of the palmar little finger but normal sensation over the dorsal medial hand and strong ulnar wrist flexion. The dorsal cutaneous branch and the branch to flexor carpi ulnaris leave the nerve in the forearm, so sparing both places the lesion at the wrist (Guyon canal). The marked claw fits, because the medial flexor digitorum profundus is intact. An elbow lesion would add weak ulnar wrist flexion and dorsal hand numbness, with a milder claw."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C03","obj":"MS-5.3","title":"Lower limb nerve injuries","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lower limb deficits are also read by compartment. The femoral nerve (L2 to L4, posterior divisions of the lumbar plexus) supplies the anterior thigh; the obturator nerve (L2 to L4, anterior divisions) supplies the medial thigh; the sciatic nerve divides into the tibial nerve for most of the posterior thigh, the posterior leg, and the sole, and the common fibular nerve for the anterior and lateral leg. The superior gluteal nerve (L4 to S1) supplies the hip abductors."},{"type":"table","title":"Lower limb nerves, muscles, and deficits","cols":["Nerve","Key muscles","Deficit","Typical cause"],"rows":[["Femoral","Quadriceps femoris, sartorius, iliacus","Weak knee extension, lost patellar reflex, numb anterior thigh and medial leg","Pelvic surgery, retroperitoneal hematoma, femoral catheterization"],["Obturator","Adductor longus, brevis, and magnus (adductor part), gracilis","Weak adduction; leg swings outward; numb medial thigh","Pelvic surgery, pelvic tumor, difficult labor"],["Superior gluteal","Gluteus medius and minimus, tensor fasciae latae","Trendelenburg sign; lurching gait","Misplaced gluteal injection, hip surgery"],["Common fibular","Anterior compartment (dorsiflexors) and lateral compartment (evertors)","Foot drop, steppage gait, weak eversion, numb dorsum of foot","Fibular neck fracture, tight cast, leg crossing"],["Tibial","Gastrocnemius, soleus, tibialis posterior, long toe flexors, sole muscles","Weak plantarflexion and inversion, cannot rise on toes, lost ankle reflex, numb sole","Popliteal fossa trauma; tarsal tunnel (distal branches)"]],"caption":"The nerve's compartment predicts the movement lost; its course predicts the cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Femoral injury makes the knee buckle on stairs or uneven ground, because the quadriceps can no longer lock it; the saphenous branch carries sensation from the medial leg. Hip flexion is only mildly affected because the psoas major receives direct branches from the lumbar plexus."},{"type":"p","text":"During walking, each limb in turn bears the body weight alone, and that weight falls medial to the stance hip. The gluteus medius and minimus of the stance limb pull on the pelvis to keep it level. When they are paralyzed by a superior gluteal nerve injury, the pelvis tilts down on the side of the lifted leg. The pelvis therefore drops on the side opposite the lesion, while the lesion is on the stance side."},{"type":"flow","title":"Trendelenburg sign from a right superior gluteal nerve injury","steps":["Patient stands on the right leg; left foot lifts","Body weight acts medial to the right hip","Right gluteus medius and minimus cannot hold the pelvis","Pelvis drops on the left (unsupported) side","Trunk lurches right to shift weight over the hip"],"caption":"Positive sign: pelvis drops opposite the weak abductors. The lurch is the compensation."},{"type":"p","text":"The common fibular nerve winds around the neck of the fibula just under the skin, which makes it the most often injured nerve of the lower limb. Its deep branch supplies the dorsiflexors and its superficial branch the evertors, so the foot hangs plantarflexed and inverted, the toes drag, and the patient lifts the knee high to clear them (steppage gait). Inversion is preserved because tibialis posterior is supplied by the tibial nerve. The tibial nerve lies deep in the popliteal fossa and is injured much less often."},{"type":"example","title":"Foot drop: common fibular nerve or L5 root?","text":"Both lesions cause foot drop and numbness over the dorsum of the foot, because the L5 root supplies the dorsiflexors through the common fibular nerve. The difference lies in muscles that receive L5 through other nerves. Tibialis posterior (tibial nerve, L4 and L5) inverts the foot, and the gluteus medius (superior gluteal nerve, L4 to S1) abducts the hip. In an L5 radiculopathy both are weak, so inversion and hip abduction are reduced, and back pain often radiates down the leg. In a fibular neck lesion inversion and hip abduction are normal, and the history usually involves the fibular neck itself (fracture, cast, prolonged leg crossing). Testing inversion is the key step."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C04","obj":"MS-5.4","title":"Head and neck muscles and their cranial nerves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Head and neck innervation follows embryologic origin. Muscles that develop from a pharyngeal arch keep that arch's nerve: first arch muscles are supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3), and second arch muscles by the facial nerve (CN VII). The tongue muscles come instead from occipital somites and migrate forward carrying the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII). The sternocleidomastoid and trapezius are supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI)."},{"type":"table","title":"Muscles of mastication (first arch, CN V3)","cols":["Muscle","Origin","Insertion","Action"],"rows":[["Masseter","Zygomatic arch","Lateral ramus and angle of mandible","Elevates mandible (closes jaw)"],["Temporalis","Temporal fossa","Coronoid process","Elevates; posterior fibers retract"],["Medial pterygoid","Lateral pterygoid plate (medial side)","Medial ramus and angle","Elevates; helps protrude and grind"],["Lateral pterygoid","Sphenoid wing and lateral pterygoid plate","Neck of mandible, TMJ disc and capsule","Protrudes and opens jaw; grinding"]],"caption":"Three muscles close the jaw; the lateral pterygoid opens it, aided by the suprahyoids and gravity."},{"type":"p","text":"Because each lateral pterygoid pulls its own condyle forward, a CN V3 lesion makes the chin deviate toward the paralyzed side on opening, as the intact muscle advances only the healthy condyle. The masseter and temporalis waste on that side, and the jaw jerk is lost. CN V3 also supplies the other first arch muscles (mylohyoid, anterior digastric, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini)."},{"type":"p","text":"The muscles of facial expression insert into skin. Key members are the frontalis (raises the eyebrows), orbicularis oculi (closes the eye), orbicularis oris (closes the lips), buccinator (keeps the cheek against the teeth so food stays between them), and platysma. CN VII also supplies the stapedius, stylohyoid, and posterior belly of digastric. A lower motor neuron lesion such as Bell palsy paralyzes the whole half of the face, including the forehead; the eye cannot close, food collects in the cheek, and loss of stapedius makes sounds seem too loud. An upper motor neuron lesion, such as a cortical stroke, spares the forehead because the part of the facial nucleus serving the upper face receives corticobulbar fibers from both hemispheres."},{"type":"p","text":"The tongue has intrinsic muscles that change its shape and extrinsic muscles that move it: genioglossus (protrudes), hyoglossus (depresses), styloglossus (retracts and elevates), and palatoglossus (elevates the back of the tongue). All are supplied by CN XII except palatoglossus, which develops with the palate muscles and is supplied by the vagus nerve (CN X) through the pharyngeal plexus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the tongue deviates toward a CN XII lesion","steps":["Each genioglossus pulls the tongue forward and toward the opposite side","Both sides acting together protrude it in the midline","One genioglossus is paralyzed","Intact genioglossus pushes the tongue toward the weak side","Lower motor neuron lesion: weak side atrophies and fasciculates"],"caption":"The protruded tongue points toward the lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"The sternocleidomastoid runs from the manubrium and medial clavicle to the mastoid process. Acting alone it turns the face to the opposite side and tilts the head toward its own shoulder; together they flex the neck. The trapezius elevates, retracts, and depresses the scapula and, with the serratus anterior, rotates it upward for overhead reach. CN XI arises from the upper cervical cord, enters the skull through the foramen magnum, and leaves through the jugular foramen. Injury in the posterior triangle, often at lymph node biopsy, spares the sternocleidomastoid (already supplied) but paralyzes the trapezius, so the shoulder droops and the arm cannot be raised fully overhead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient cannot shrug the right shoulder and has difficulty turning the head to the left. Where along CN XI is the lesion likely to be?","explain":"Proximal, at or near the jugular foramen. The right sternocleidomastoid turns the face to the left, so its weakness plus trapezius weakness means the nerve was injured before it supplied the sternocleidomastoid. A posterior triangle lesion would spare head turning.","check":"The sternocleidomastoid and CN XI paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C05","obj":"MS-5.5","title":"Trunk muscles: back, diaphragm, abdominal wall, and pelvic floor","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Trunk muscles fall into two developmental groups. Epaxial muscles form behind the vertebral column and are supplied by dorsal rami; hypaxial muscles form the body wall in front and are supplied by ventral rami. The erector spinae is the main epaxial group. Its three columns (iliocostalis laterally, longissimus, spinalis medially) arise by a common tendon from the posterior iliac crest, sacrum, and lumbar spinous processes and insert on the ribs, vertebrae, and skull. Acting together they extend the spine; acting on one side they bend it laterally. Superficial back muscles such as the trapezius and latissimus dorsi are limb muscles that migrated onto the back and are not supplied by dorsal rami."},{"type":"table","title":"Trunk muscles at a glance","cols":["Muscle","Main action","Innervation"],"rows":[["Erector spinae","Extends and laterally flexes the spine","Dorsal rami of spinal nerves"],["Diaphragm","Main muscle of inspiration","Phrenic nerve (C3 to C5)"],["External oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis","Compress abdomen; flex and rotate trunk","Thoracoabdominal (T7 to T11) and subcostal (T12); L1 branches to the lowest internal oblique and transversus"],["Rectus abdominis","Flexes trunk; compresses abdomen","Thoracoabdominal and subcostal nerves"],["Levator ani (puborectalis, pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus)","Supports pelvic viscera; maintains anorectal angle","Nerve to levator ani (S4) and pudendal branches"]],"caption":"Dorsal rami supply the deep back; ventral rami supply the rest."},{"type":"p","text":"The diaphragm arises from the xiphoid process, the lower six costal cartilages, and the lumbar vertebrae (through the crura and arcuate ligaments), and inserts into its own central tendon. When it contracts, the dome descends and the lower ribs are raised, enlarging the thorax, lowering intrapleural pressure, and drawing air in. In quiet breathing it generates most of the tidal volume. Its motor supply is the phrenic nerve from C3 to C5, mainly C4, because its muscle precursors arise at cervical levels and descend with their nerve. The phrenic nerve is also sensory to the central diaphragm, so irritation there refers pain to the shoulder (C3 and C4 dermatomes)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cervical cord level and breathing","steps":["Complete injury at or above C3: phrenic output lost, apnea","Injury at C4 to C5: diaphragm partly spared, ventilation impaired","Injury below C5: diaphragm works, intercostals and abdominals lost","Below C5: weak cough and forced expiration"],"caption":"Unilateral phrenic palsy instead gives an elevated hemidiaphragm that moves paradoxically upward on a sniff."},{"type":"p","text":"The anterolateral abdominal wall has three flat layers with crossing fiber directions (external oblique running inferomedially, internal oblique superomedially, transversus abdominis horizontally) and the vertical rectus abdominis in its sheath. Contracting together, they raise intra-abdominal pressure for forced expiration, coughing, defecation, and childbirth. Their nerves are the continuations of the lower intercostal nerves (thoracoabdominal, T7 to T11) and the subcostal nerve (T12), with the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves (L1) to the lowest fibers. These run between the internal oblique and transversus, so supply and skin sensation are segmental, with T10 at the umbilicus."},{"type":"p","text":"The levator ani is a thin muscular sheet stretching from the pubis, the tendinous arch over the obturator internus fascia, and the ischial spine to the perineal body, anococcygeal body, and coccyx. Its tone supports the bladder, uterus, and rectum, and it contracts reflexly whenever intra-abdominal pressure rises. The puborectalis slings the anorectal junction forward, holding the anorectal angle that contributes to fecal continence; it relaxes during defecation. Stretching or tearing of the levator ani and its nerves during vaginal delivery is a major cause of pelvic organ prolapse and stress urinary incontinence."},{"type":"stop","predict":"During a hard cough, why does urine leak in a woman with a weakened pelvic floor but not in one with a healthy pelvic floor?","explain":"The abdominal wall and diaphragm raise intra-abdominal pressure. Normally the levator ani contracts at the same moment, supporting the bladder neck and urethra so urethral closure pressure rises with bladder pressure. Without that support, bladder pressure exceeds urethral pressure and urine leaks.","check":"The levator ani paragraph and the abdominal wall actions."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology together explain innervation. Arch muscles keep the arch nerve (V3, VII, and the vagus for the palate and pharynx), somite-derived tongue muscles keep CN XII, epaxial back muscles keep dorsal rami, and the diaphragm keeps the cervical phrenic nerve after descending. In the limbs, compartments developed as dorsal and ventral muscle masses supplied by posterior and anterior divisions of the plexuses, which is why one nerve serves one functional group and why a single deficit localizes so well. Neuroscience adds the next layer: a peripheral nerve lesion affects one nerve's muscles and skin, a root lesion affects every muscle sharing that segment across several nerves, and an upper motor neuron lesion weakens movements with spasticity and brisk reflexes rather than wasting."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology supplies the mechanisms of injury: compression at fixed tunnels (carpal tunnel, suprascapular notch, fibular neck), traction or laceration during fractures and surgery, degeneration of poorly vascularized tendons, and stretching of the pelvic floor in childbirth. Physiology explains the consequences, from the lost force couple in a rotator cuff tear to hypoventilation after phrenic injury. Real anatomy varies, so the classic patterns described here are the starting point for reasoning, and electrodiagnostic testing or imaging confirms the site in practice."}],"summary":["The rotator cuff holds the humeral head in the shallow glenoid; supraspinatus initiates abduction, infraspinatus and teres minor rotate laterally, and subscapularis rotates medially.","Suprascapular nerve: supraspinatus and infraspinatus. Axillary nerve: teres minor and deltoid. Supraspinatus is torn most often because it is compressed under the coracoacromial arch.","Upper limb: musculocutaneous loss weakens elbow flexion, radial loss causes wrist drop, median loss at the wrist causes ape hand, and ulnar loss causes clawing of digits 4 and 5.","Claw hand results from paralyzed lumbricals and interossei, which leaves MCP joints hyperextended and IP joints flexed.","Long thoracic nerve injury paralyzes serratus anterior and wings the scapula; it is a classic complication of axillary surgery.","Lower limb: femoral loss weakens knee extension, obturator loss weakens adduction, common fibular loss causes foot drop with weak eversion, and tibial loss weakens plantarflexion and inversion.","In a Trendelenburg sign the pelvis drops on the lifted side; the weak gluteus medius and minimus are on the stance side.","CN V3 supplies mastication, CN VII facial expression, CN XII the tongue except palatoglossus (CN X), and CN XI the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius.","Diaphragm: phrenic nerve C3 to C5; complete cord injury at or above C3 causes apnea. Abdominal wall: T7 to T12 and L1 in a segmental pattern.","Erector spinae extends the spine on dorsal rami; levator ani supports the pelvic organs, and puborectalis holds the anorectal angle."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb: shoulder region, brachial plexus, and nerve injuries"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb: gluteal region, thigh, leg, and nerve injuries"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head and Neck: muscles of mastication, face, tongue, and the accessory nerve"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back, Abdomen, and Pelvis and Perineum: intrinsic back muscles, diaphragm, anterolateral abdominal wall, and pelvic floor"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck (pharyngeal arches and tongue); Muscular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation"}]}});
